WEBVTT

00:00:03.550 --> 00:00:04.480
-

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:33.040
Hello there, Mr. Neff.

00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:47.770
You're working pretty late, aren't you, Mr. Neff?

00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:49.650
Wait enough. Let's ride.

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:55.220
You look kind of all-in at that.

00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:56.290
Fine.

00:02:56.500 --> 00:02:58.710
How's the insurance business, Mr. Neff?

00:02:58.740 --> 00:02:59.730
Okay.

00:03:00.510 --> 00:03:02.230
They wouldn't ever sell me any.

00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:04.360
They said I had something loose in my heart.

00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:06.740
I say it's rheumatism.

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:09.600
- Sure? - Twelve.

00:05:05.630 --> 00:05:10.560
Office memorandum, old enough to Barton Keyes, claims manager.

00:05:12.550 --> 00:05:15.040
Los Angeles, July 16th, 1938.

00:05:16.780 --> 00:05:21.640
Dear Keyes, I suppose you'll call this a confession when you hear it.

00:05:22.990 --> 00:05:24.480
Well, I don't like the word confession.

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:29.060
I just want to set you right about something you couldn't see because it was smack up against your nose.

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:32.480
You think you're such a hot potato as a claims manager,

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:34.100
such a wolf on a phony claim.

00:05:35.810 --> 00:05:36.350
Maybe you are.

00:05:37.580 --> 00:05:39.410
But let's take a look at that Diedrickson claim.

00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:42.220
Accident and double indemnity.

00:05:44.260 --> 00:05:45.660
You were pretty good in there for a while, Keyes.

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:48.380
You said it wasn't an accident.

00:05:48.860 --> 00:05:49.100
Check.

00:05:50.510 --> 00:05:51.370
You said it wasn't suicide.

00:05:52.110 --> 00:05:52.370
Check.

00:05:53.850 --> 00:05:54.550
You said it was murder.

00:05:56.700 --> 00:05:56.940
Check.

00:05:59.050 --> 00:06:00.000
You thought you had it cold, didn't you?

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:04.110
All wrapped up in tissue paper with pink ribbons around it.

00:06:05.070 --> 00:06:06.830
It was perfect.

00:06:07.150 --> 00:06:09.060
Except it wasn't because you made one mistake.

00:06:09.970 --> 00:06:11.730
Just one little mistake.

00:06:13.290 --> 00:06:16.370
When it came to picking the killer, you picked the wrong guy.

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:19.790
You want to know who killed Dietrichsen?

00:06:21.640 --> 00:06:25.390
Hold tight to that cheap cigar of yours, Keyes.

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:27.550
I killed Dietrichsen.

00:06:27.650 --> 00:06:30.000
Me, Walter Neff. Insurance.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:33.190
35 years old, unmarried, no visible scars

00:06:33.190 --> 00:06:37.000
Until a while ago, that is

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:40.330
Yes, I killed him

00:06:40.330 --> 00:06:43.620
I killed him for money

00:06:43.620 --> 00:06:45.530
And for a woman

00:06:45.530 --> 00:06:49.000
And I didn't get the money

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:52.720
And I didn't get the woman

00:06:52.720 --> 00:06:55.860
Pretty, isn't it?

00:07:11.130 --> 00:07:15.470
It all began last May, but on the end of May, it was.

00:07:17.670 --> 00:07:20.470
I'd been out to Glendale to deliver a policy on some dairy trucks.

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:24.720
On the way back, I remembered this auto-renewal near Los Feliz Boulevard.

00:07:25.090 --> 00:07:26.210
So I drove over there.

00:07:27.590 --> 00:07:30.000
It was one of those California Spanish houses everyone was nuts.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:32.790
- That's about 10 or 15 years ago.

00:07:32.790 --> 00:07:36.170
This one must have cost somebody about 30,000 bucks.

00:07:36.170 --> 00:07:40.280
That is, if you ever finish paying for it.

00:07:40.280 --> 00:07:42.480
- Mr. Diedrickson.

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:43.400
- Who wants to see him?

00:07:43.400 --> 00:07:45.300
- The name is Neff, Walter Neff.

00:07:45.300 --> 00:07:46.640
- If you're selling something.

00:07:46.640 --> 00:07:48.100
- Look, it's Mr. Diedrickson I want to talk to,

00:07:48.100 --> 00:07:49.300
and it's not magazine subscription.

00:07:49.300 --> 00:07:51.220
- Listen, Mr. Diedrickson is not in.

00:07:51.220 --> 00:07:52.220
- How soon do you expect him?

00:07:52.220 --> 00:07:54.220
- He'll be home when he gets you, if that's any help to you.

00:07:54.220 --> 00:07:56.200
- What is it, Nettie, who is it?

00:07:56.200 --> 00:07:58.070
- It's for Mr. Diedrickson.

00:07:58.070 --> 00:08:00.000
- I'm Mrs. Diedrickson, what is it?

00:08:00.760 --> 00:08:02.090
How do you do, Mr. Diedrickson?

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:03.940
I'm Walter Neff, Pacific All-Risk.

00:08:03.940 --> 00:08:05.160
Pacific All-what?

00:08:05.170 --> 00:08:07.140
The Pacific All-Risk Insurance Company.

00:08:07.260 --> 00:08:09.160
It's about some renewals on the automobiles.

00:08:09.720 --> 00:08:11.420
I've been trying to contact your husband for the past two weeks,

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:12.880
but he's never in his office.

00:08:13.260 --> 00:08:14.660
Is there anything I can do?

00:08:15.550 --> 00:08:16.850
The insurance ran out on the 15th.

00:08:16.950 --> 00:08:18.950
I'd hate to think of your having a smashed fender or something

00:08:19.050 --> 00:08:21.700
while you're not fully covered.

00:08:22.790 --> 00:08:25.030
Perhaps I know what you mean, Mr. Neff.

00:08:25.560 --> 00:08:27.400
I've just been taking a sunbed.

00:08:27.640 --> 00:08:29.160
No pigeons around, I hope.

00:08:30.540 --> 00:08:33.580
Now, about those policies, Mrs. Diedrickson, I hate to take up your time, but...

00:08:33.580 --> 00:08:34.860
Oh, that's all right.

00:08:34.860 --> 00:08:37.340
If you wait till I put something on, I'll be right down.

00:08:37.340 --> 00:08:42.480
Nettie, show Mr. Neff into the living room.

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:45.190
- Where would the living room be? - In there.

00:08:45.190 --> 00:08:47.090
But they keep the liquor locked up.

00:08:47.090 --> 00:08:50.850
It's all right, Al. I'll just carry my own keys.

00:08:50.850 --> 00:09:00.000
The living room was still stuffy from last night's cigars.

00:09:01.880 --> 00:09:05.720
the windows were closed and the sunshine coming in through the venetian blind showed up the dust in

00:09:05.720 --> 00:09:13.110
the air on the piano and a couple of fancy frames were mr dietrichson and lola his daughter by his

00:09:13.110 --> 00:09:19.490
first wife they had a bowl of those little red goldfish on the table behind the big davenport

00:09:19.490 --> 00:09:24.480
but to tell you the truth keys i wasn't a whole lot interested in goldfish right then

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:30.000
or an auto renewals or on mr dietrichson and his daughter lola i was thinking about the

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:31.940
that dame upstairs and the way she had looked at me.

00:09:33.110 --> 00:09:34.730
And I wanted to see her again, close,

00:09:35.560 --> 00:09:37.760
without that silly staircase between us.

00:09:39.280 --> 00:09:45.100
How long was I?

00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:46.280
Not at all, Mrs. Dietrichson.

00:09:46.460 --> 00:09:47.860
Hope I've got my face on straight.

00:09:48.430 --> 00:09:49.480
Perfect for my money.

00:09:50.030 --> 00:09:50.950
Neff is the name, isn't it?

00:09:51.150 --> 00:09:53.620
Yeah, two Fs, like in Philadelphia, if you know the story.

00:09:53.970 --> 00:09:54.550
What story?

00:09:54.810 --> 00:09:55.510
Philadelphia story.

00:09:56.490 --> 00:09:58.450
Suppose we sit down and you tell me about the insurance.

00:09:58.690 --> 00:10:00.000
My husband never tells me anything.

00:10:01.240 --> 00:10:03.940
Well, it's on your two cars, the LaSalle and the Plymouth.

00:10:05.820 --> 00:10:08.600
We've been handling this insurance for Mr. Diedrickson for three years...

00:10:08.680 --> 00:10:10.440
and we'd hate to see the policies lapse.

00:10:10.830 --> 00:10:14.670
That's a honey of an ankle that you're wearing, Mr. Diedrickson.

00:10:16.040 --> 00:10:19.080
As I was saying, we'd hate to see the policies lapse.

00:10:19.180 --> 00:10:21.420
Of course, we give him 30 days. That's all we're allowed to give.

00:10:21.500 --> 00:10:24.230
I guess he's been too busy down at Long Beach in the oil fields.

00:10:24.230 --> 00:10:25.920
Couldn't I catch him at home some evening for a few minutes?

00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:28.120
I suppose so, but he's never home much before eight.

00:10:28.200 --> 00:10:29.250
That's fine with me.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:33.900
- You're not connected with the automobile club, are you? - No, the all-risk, Mrs. Diedrickson. Why?

00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:36.950
Somebody from the automobile club has been trying to get them.

00:10:37.190 --> 00:10:39.700
- Did they have a better rate? - If your husband's a member.

00:10:39.850 --> 00:10:42.580
- No, he isn't. - Well, then he'd have to join the club

00:10:42.680 --> 00:10:44.210
and pay the membership fee to start with.

00:10:44.560 --> 00:10:47.000
I never knock the other fellow's merchandise, Mrs. Diedrickson.

00:10:47.640 --> 00:10:51.000
The automobile club's fine. I can do just as well for you, though.

00:10:51.300 --> 00:10:53.400
I have a very attractive policy here.

00:10:53.770 --> 00:10:55.990
Wouldn't take me two minutes to put it in front of your husband.

00:10:56.490 --> 00:10:59.860
For instance, for writing a new kind of 50% retention feature in the collision coverage.

00:11:01.720 --> 00:11:05.750
- You're a smart insurance man, aren't you, Mr. Neff? - Well, I've been at it eleven years.

00:11:05.750 --> 00:11:08.930
- Doing pretty well? - It's a living.

00:11:08.930 --> 00:11:13.350
- You handle just automobile insurance or all kinds? - All kinds.

00:11:13.350 --> 00:11:18.400
Fire, earthquake, theft, public liability, group insurance, industrial stuff and so on right down the line.

00:11:18.400 --> 00:11:23.550
- Accident insurance? - Accident insurance? Sure, Mr. Diedrickson.

00:11:23.550 --> 00:11:30.000
- Wish you'd tell me what's engraved on that anklet. - Just my name.

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:35.000
- Tell us. - Tell us, huh? I think I like that.

00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:39.040
- But you're not sure? - I'd have to drive it around the block a couple of times.

00:11:39.040 --> 00:11:44.050
Mr. Neff, why don't you drop by tomorrow evening around 8:30. He'll be in then.

00:11:44.050 --> 00:11:48.360
- Who? - My husband. You were anxious to talk to him, weren't you?

00:11:48.360 --> 00:11:51.910
Yeah, I was, but I'm sort of getting over the idea, if you know what I mean.

00:11:51.910 --> 00:11:55.870
There's a speed limit in this state, Mr. Neff. Forty-five miles an hour.

00:11:55.870 --> 00:11:59.510
- How fast was I going, officer? - I'd say around 90.

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:04.710
- Suppose you get down off your motorcycle and give me a ticket? - Suppose I let you off with a warning this time.

00:12:04.710 --> 00:12:08.960
- Suppose it doesn't take. - Suppose I have to whack you over the knuckles.

00:12:08.960 --> 00:12:15.330
- Suppose I bust out crying and put my head on your shoulder. - Suppose you try putting it on my husband's shoulder.

00:12:15.330 --> 00:12:24.530
- That tears it. 8:30 tomorrow evening, then. - That's what I suggested.

00:12:24.530 --> 00:12:27.190
- Would you be here, too? - I guess so. I usually am.

00:12:27.190 --> 00:12:30.000
- Same chair, same perfume, same anklet? - I wonder if...

00:12:30.030 --> 00:12:34.510
- What if I know what you mean? - I wonder if you wonder.

00:12:34.510 --> 00:12:43.700
It was a hot afternoon and I can still remember the smell of honeysuckle all along that street.

00:12:43.700 --> 00:12:47.610
How could I have known that murder can sometimes smell like honeysuckle?

00:12:47.610 --> 00:12:51.380
Maybe you would have known, Keyes, the minute she mentioned accident insurance.

00:12:51.380 --> 00:12:55.110
But I didn't. I felt like a million.

00:12:55.110 --> 00:13:00.000
I went back to the office to see if I had any mail. It was the day you had that truck

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:03.600
I'm a truck driver from Inglewood on the carpet. Remember, Keyes?

00:13:03.780 --> 00:13:10.000
- Jeanette, Mr. Keyes wants to see you. He's been yelling for you all afternoon. - Is he sore? Just frothing at the mouth a little.

00:13:10.090 --> 00:13:23.680
- Park this for me, will you, sweetheart? - Come on, come on, Garlopers. You're not kidding anybody with that line of balls.

00:13:23.740 --> 00:13:29.510
- You're in the jam and you know it. - Says you, "All I want is my money." - Says you? All you're gonna get is the cops.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:34.080
- Hello, Walter. This is Sam Garlopes from Inglewood. - Oh, sure, I know Mr. Garlopes.

00:13:34.080 --> 00:13:37.180
- Wrote a policy on his truck. - How are you, Mr. Garlopes?

00:13:37.180 --> 00:13:42.190
- I ain't so good. My truck burned down. - Yeah. Now look, Garlopes.

00:13:42.190 --> 00:13:44.720
Every month, hundreds of claims come to this desk.

00:13:44.720 --> 00:13:47.140
Some of them are phonies, and I know which ones.

00:13:47.140 --> 00:13:50.800
How do I know? Because my little man tells me.

00:13:50.800 --> 00:13:54.750
- What little man? - The little man in here.

00:13:54.750 --> 00:13:59.060
Every time one of these phonies comes along, he ties knots in my stomach. I can't eat.

00:13:59.060 --> 00:14:00.000
Yours was one of them, Garlopes.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:02.840
- That's how I knew your clay was crooked. - So what did I do?

00:14:02.940 --> 00:14:06.070
I sent a tow car over to your garage this afternoon.

00:14:06.170 --> 00:14:09.250
And they jacked up that burned-out truck of yours. And what did they find?

00:14:09.350 --> 00:14:12.180
They found what was left of a neat pile of shavings.

00:14:12.280 --> 00:14:17.860
- What shavings? - The ones you soak with kerosene and drop the match on.

00:14:17.960 --> 00:14:24.340
Look, mister, I'm just a poor guy. Maybe I made a mistake.

00:14:24.440 --> 00:14:25.990
That's one way of putting it.

00:14:26.090 --> 00:14:30.000
- I'm feeling so good, Mr. Kees. - Just a minute.

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:32.400
- You sign this and you'll feel fine. - Sign what?

00:14:32.600 --> 00:14:34.520
It's a waiver on your claim, right here.

00:14:35.870 --> 00:14:36.820
- Here? - Here.

00:14:40.670 --> 00:14:42.070
Now you're an honest man again.

00:14:42.270 --> 00:14:43.220
Goodbye, Carl Lopez.

00:14:44.000 --> 00:14:45.560
But I ain't got no more truck.

00:14:45.970 --> 00:14:48.570
Twenty-six hundred bucks is allowed to know where I live.

00:14:48.770 --> 00:14:50.770
What's the matter, Carl Lopez? Don't you know how to open the door?

00:14:50.920 --> 00:14:53.750
Just put your hand on the knob, turn it to the left.

00:14:54.340 --> 00:14:59.960
Now pull it toward you. That's the boy.

00:15:00.640 --> 00:15:02.780
Thank you, Mr. Keyes.

00:15:02.780 --> 00:15:07.680
What kind of an outfit is this, anyway?

00:15:07.680 --> 00:15:11.720
Are we an insurance company or just a bunch of dim-witted amateurs to write a policy on a mug like that?

00:15:11.720 --> 00:15:13.540
Now, wait a minute, Keyes. I don't rate this beef.

00:15:13.540 --> 00:15:17.540
I clipped a note of that Garlopa's application to have him thoroughly investigated before we accepted the risk.

00:15:17.540 --> 00:15:19.540
I know you did, Walter. I'm not beefing at you. It's the company. It's the way they do things.

00:15:19.540 --> 00:15:21.440
The way they don't do things.

00:15:21.440 --> 00:15:24.500
The way they'll write anything just to get it down on the sales sheet.

00:15:24.500 --> 00:15:27.510
And I'm the guy that asked this idiot up to my neck and phony claims

00:15:27.510 --> 00:15:30.000
that they won't throw more money out the window than they take in at the door.

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:38.730
- Okay, turn the record over, let's see who the other side is. - Well, I get darn sick of trying to pick up after a gang of fast-talking salesmen dumb enough to sell life insurance to a guy who sleeps in the same bed with four rattlesnakes.

00:15:38.730 --> 00:15:43.190
Walter, I've had 26 years of this, and let me tell you, I'm getting... - Yeah, and you loved every minute of it, Keyes.

00:15:43.190 --> 00:15:50.030
You love it, only you worry about it too darn much. You and your little man. You're so darn conscientious, you're driving yourself crazy.

00:15:50.030 --> 00:15:55.440
You wouldn't even say today's Tuesday unless you look at the calendar. Then you check to see if it was this year's or last year's calendar.

00:15:55.440 --> 00:16:00.000
Then you find out who printed the calendar and find out if their calendar checked with the World Almanac's calendar. - Now, that's nothing.

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:29.890
from you, Walter. Now, get out of here before I throw my desk at you. I love you, too. I really did, too, you old crab. I was yelling your head off. I always saw it, everybody. But you never fooled me with your song and dance, not for a second. I kind of always knew that behind the cigar ashes on your vest, you had a heart as big as a house.

00:16:31.630 --> 00:16:35.350
Back in my office, there was a phone message from Mrs. Dietrichsen about the renewals.

00:16:36.110 --> 00:16:38.100
She didn't want me to come tomorrow evening.

00:16:39.010 --> 00:16:42.880
She wanted me to come Thursday afternoon at 3:30 instead.

00:16:43.430 --> 00:16:46.040
I had a lot of stuff lined up for that Thursday afternoon,

00:16:46.680 --> 00:16:52.140
including a trip down to Santa Monica to see a couple of live prospects about some group insurance.

00:16:52.730 --> 00:16:58.960
But I kept thinking about Phyllis Dietrichsen and the way that anklet of hers cut into her leg.

00:17:05.760 --> 00:17:10.310
- Hello, Mr. Neff. Aren't you coming in? - I'm considering it.

00:17:13.170 --> 00:17:17.000
I hope you didn't mind my change on the appointment. Last night wasn't so convenient.

00:17:17.230 --> 00:17:19.970
It's all right. I was working on my staff collection anyway.

00:17:20.550 --> 00:17:22.990
I was just fixing some iced tea. Would you like a glass?

00:17:23.190 --> 00:17:24.770
Unless you've got a bottle of beer that's not working.

00:17:24.960 --> 00:17:27.360
There may be some. I never know what's in the icebox.

00:17:27.820 --> 00:17:28.610
Nettie!

00:17:33.230 --> 00:17:36.470
Oh, about those renewals, Mr. Neff, I talked to my husband about it.

00:17:36.650 --> 00:17:39.250
- Oh, you did? - Yes, he'll renew with you. He told me so.

00:17:39.450 --> 00:17:41.810
As a matter of fact, I thought he'd be here this afternoon.

00:17:42.040 --> 00:17:43.350
- But he's not. - No.

00:17:44.500 --> 00:17:46.480
It's terrible.

00:17:46.830 --> 00:17:48.950
Nettie! Nettie!

00:17:49.620 --> 00:17:51.640
Oh, I forgot. Today's the maid's day off.

00:17:53.180 --> 00:17:55.440
Never mind the beer. Ice tea will be fine.

00:17:56.130 --> 00:17:57.930
- Lemon? Sugar? - Fix it your way.

00:18:00.090 --> 00:18:03.070
As long as it's the maid's day off, maybe there's something I can do for you.

00:18:03.760 --> 00:18:06.820
- Like running the vacuum cleaner. - Fresh.

00:18:06.820 --> 00:18:08.910
These are pedal vacuum cleaners.

00:18:09.800 --> 00:18:11.670
Not much money, but you learn a lot about life.

00:18:11.750 --> 00:18:14.560
I didn't think you learned it from a correspondence course.

00:18:16.860 --> 00:18:18.550
Where'd you pick up this tea-drinking?

00:18:19.200 --> 00:18:21.480
- You're not English, are you? - Oh, Californian.

00:18:21.560 --> 00:18:23.140
Born right here in Los Angeles.

00:18:23.540 --> 00:18:25.860
They say all native Californians come from Iowa.

00:18:27.710 --> 00:18:29.890
- Mr. Neff, I... - Make it Warner, huh?

00:18:30.570 --> 00:18:33.490
Walter. That's right. Tell me, Walter,

00:18:33.650 --> 00:18:34.750
on this insurance,

00:18:35.560 --> 00:18:37.850
how much commission do you make? 20%.

00:18:37.850 --> 00:18:39.580
Why? Oh, I thought perhaps

00:18:39.580 --> 00:18:41.320
I could throw a little more business your way.

00:18:41.440 --> 00:18:43.450
I can always use it. I was thinking about my

00:18:43.450 --> 00:18:45.450
husband. I worry a lot about him down

00:18:45.450 --> 00:18:47.650
in those oil fields. It's very dangerous.

00:18:47.910 --> 00:18:49.410
Well, not for an executive, is it? Oh, he

00:18:49.410 --> 00:18:51.350
doesn't just sit behind a desk. He's right down there

00:18:51.350 --> 00:18:53.620
with those drilling crews. It's got me worried

00:18:53.620 --> 00:18:55.720
sick. You mean some dark night, a crown

00:18:55.720 --> 00:18:57.740
block might fall on him? Please don't talk like that.

00:18:57.820 --> 00:19:00.000
But that's the idea. The other day, a casing

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:03.040
- He's in the hospital with a broken back. - That's bad.

00:19:03.140 --> 00:19:06.160
It's got me jittery just thinking about it.

00:19:06.260 --> 00:19:09.300
- Suppose something like that happened to my husband? - It could.

00:19:09.400 --> 00:19:14.240
Well, don't you think he ought to have accident insurance?

00:19:14.340 --> 00:19:18.840
- Mm-hmm. - What kind of insurance could he have?

00:19:18.940 --> 00:19:21.200
Oh, enough to cover doctors and hospital bills.

00:19:21.300 --> 00:19:23.250
Say, $125 a week cash benefit.

00:19:23.350 --> 00:19:25.210
At any rate around $50,000 capital sum.

00:19:25.310 --> 00:19:28.740
- Capital sum? What's that? - In case he gets killed.

00:19:28.840 --> 00:19:29.190
build.

00:19:30.830 --> 00:19:32.820
Maybe I shouldn't have said that.

00:19:33.270 --> 00:19:35.570
I suppose you have to think of everything in your business.

00:19:35.670 --> 00:19:37.050
Well, your husband would understand.

00:19:37.130 --> 00:19:39.790
I'm sure I could sell him on the idea of some accident protection.

00:19:40.010 --> 00:19:41.210
Why don't I talk to him about it?

00:19:41.330 --> 00:19:43.760
You could try, but he's pretty tough going.

00:19:43.880 --> 00:19:45.080
Well, they're all tough at first.

00:19:46.990 --> 00:19:48.090
He has a lot on his mind.

00:19:48.190 --> 00:19:51.990
He doesn't seem to want to listen to anything except maybe a baseball game on the radio.

00:19:53.000 --> 00:19:57.000
Sometimes we sit here all evening and never say a word to each other.

00:19:57.230 --> 00:19:58.400
Sounds pretty dull.

00:19:58.920 --> 00:20:00.000
So I just sit.

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:00.640
and knit.

00:20:01.860 --> 00:20:02.620
Is that what you

00:20:02.620 --> 00:20:03.100
married him for?

00:20:03.440 --> 00:20:04.280
Maybe I like the way

00:20:04.280 --> 00:20:05.120
his thumbs hold up

00:20:05.120 --> 00:20:05.540
the wool.

00:20:07.040 --> 00:20:07.760
Anytime his thumbs

00:20:07.760 --> 00:20:08.340
get tired,

00:20:10.030 --> 00:20:10.610
only with me around

00:20:10.610 --> 00:20:10.990
you wouldn't have

00:20:10.990 --> 00:20:11.270
to knit.

00:20:11.880 --> 00:20:12.340
Wouldn't I?

00:20:12.650 --> 00:20:13.250
I bet your life

00:20:13.250 --> 00:20:13.510
you would.

00:20:17.140 --> 00:20:17.720
What if little Roma

00:20:17.720 --> 00:20:18.100
would get this

00:20:18.100 --> 00:20:18.640
up on its feet?

00:20:21.160 --> 00:20:23.660
I want to ask you

00:20:23.660 --> 00:20:24.420
something, Walter.

00:20:25.570 --> 00:20:28.100
Could I get an

00:20:28.100 --> 00:20:29.170
accident policy for

00:20:29.170 --> 00:20:30.000
him without bothering

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:33.580
him at all. How's that again? It would make it easier for you, too. You wouldn't even have to

00:20:33.580 --> 00:20:38.960
talk to him. I have a little allowance of my own. I could pay for it, and he needn't know anything

00:20:38.960 --> 00:20:47.230
about it. Why shouldn't he know? Because he doesn't want accident insurance. He's superstitious about

00:20:47.230 --> 00:20:52.950
it. A lot of people are, and it's funny, isn't it? If there was a way to get it like that, all the worry

00:20:52.950 --> 00:21:00.000
would be over. See what I mean, Walter? Sure, I got good eyesight. You mean you want him to have the policy without

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:03.400
knowing it. That means without the insurance company knowing that he doesn't know it.

00:21:03.400 --> 00:21:04.830
That's the set-up, isn't it?

00:21:04.830 --> 00:21:07.100
Is there anything wrong with it?

00:21:07.100 --> 00:21:10.310
No, I think it's lovely. Then, if some dark, wet night, that crown block did fall on him.

00:21:10.310 --> 00:21:11.310
What crown block?

00:21:11.310 --> 00:21:14.310
Only sometimes it can't quite make it on its own. It has to have a little help.

00:21:14.310 --> 00:21:16.810
I don't know what you're talking about.

00:21:16.810 --> 00:21:20.740
Of course, it doesn't have to be a crown block. It can be a car backing over and we could fall out of the upstairs window.

00:21:20.740 --> 00:21:23.210
Any little thing like that, just so it's a morgue job.

00:21:23.210 --> 00:21:24.190
Are you crazy?

00:21:24.190 --> 00:21:28.820
Not that crazy. Goodbye, Mrs. Diedrickson.

00:21:28.820 --> 00:21:29.760
What's the matter?

00:21:32.200 --> 00:21:37.780
You can't get away with it. You'll knock him off, don't you?

00:21:38.020 --> 00:21:39.670
That's a horrible thing to say.

00:21:39.760 --> 00:21:41.100
What do you think I was, anyway?

00:21:41.250 --> 00:21:42.850
A guy that walks into a good-looking dame's front parlor...

00:21:42.950 --> 00:21:45.440
and says, "Good afternoon, I sell accident insurance on husbands"?

00:21:45.530 --> 00:21:48.440
You got one that's been around too long, one you'd like to turn into a little hard cash?

00:21:48.540 --> 00:21:50.220
Just give me a smile and I'll help you collect.

00:21:50.620 --> 00:21:52.290
Boy, what a dope you must think I am.

00:21:52.430 --> 00:21:53.660
I think you're rotten.

00:21:54.070 --> 00:21:56.450
I think you're swell, so long as I'm not your husband.

00:21:56.550 --> 00:21:57.620
Get out of here.

00:21:57.730 --> 00:22:00.000
You bet I'll get out of here, baby. I'll get out of here.

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:13.770
So I let her have it straight between the eyes.

00:22:13.770 --> 00:22:15.890
She didn't fool me for a minute, not this time.

00:22:15.890 --> 00:22:20.580
I knew I had hold of a red hot poker and the time to drop her was before it burned my hand off.

00:22:20.580 --> 00:22:25.270
I stopped at a drive-in for a bottle of beer, the one I had wanted all along.

00:22:25.270 --> 00:22:29.700
Only I wanted it worse now to get rid of the sour taste of her iced tea.

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:36.720
I didn't want to go back to the office, so I dropped by a bowling alley at Third and Western and rolled a few lines.

00:22:36.720 --> 00:22:40.090
To get my mind thinking about something else for a while.

00:22:40.090 --> 00:22:44.290
I didn't feel like eating dinner when I left and I didn't feel like a show.

00:22:44.290 --> 00:22:48.950
So I drove home, put the car away, and went up to my apartment.

00:22:48.950 --> 00:22:54.730
It had begun to rain outside and I watched it get dark and didn't even turn on the light.

00:22:54.730 --> 00:23:00.000
That didn't help me either. I was all twisted up inside and I was terrified.

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:02.910
I was still holding on to that red hot poker.

00:23:02.910 --> 00:23:05.910
And right then it came over me that I hadn't walked out on anything at all.

00:23:05.910 --> 00:23:08.760
That the hook was too strong.

00:23:08.760 --> 00:23:11.190
That this wasn't the end between her and me.

00:23:11.190 --> 00:23:14.500
It was only the beginning.

00:23:14.500 --> 00:23:18.700
So at 8 o'clock the bell would ring and I'd know who it was without even having to think.

00:23:18.700 --> 00:23:21.410
As if it was the most natural thing in the world.

00:23:21.410 --> 00:23:29.060
Hello. I forgot your hat this afternoon.

00:23:30.860 --> 00:23:36.750
- Don't you want me to bring it in? - Sure. Put it on the chair.

00:23:36.790 --> 00:23:48.400
- How do you know where I live? - It's in the phone book.

00:23:48.880 --> 00:23:51.280
- It's raining. - Yeah.

00:23:51.280 --> 00:23:53.620
Peel off your coat and sit down.

00:23:54.010 --> 00:24:00.000
- Your husband out? - Yes, Long Beach. They're spreading it in New York.

00:24:00.090 --> 00:24:02.740
He phoned and he'd be late, about 9:30.

00:24:02.790 --> 00:24:05.760
It's about time you said you were glad to see me.

00:24:06.120 --> 00:24:07.490
I knew you wouldn't leave it like that.

00:24:07.680 --> 00:24:10.470
- Like what? - Like it was this afternoon.

00:24:10.510 --> 00:24:13.440
I must have said something that gave you a terribly wrong impression.

00:24:13.500 --> 00:24:16.550
You must never think anything like that about me, Walter.

00:24:16.550 --> 00:24:19.640
- Okay? - No, it's not okay. Not if you don't believe me.

00:24:19.650 --> 00:24:21.160
What do you want me to do?

00:24:21.760 --> 00:24:26.720
I want you to be nice to me, like the first time you came to the house.

00:24:27.010 --> 00:24:28.710
Can't be like the first time.

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:34.010
I know it has. It's happened to us.

00:24:34.010 --> 00:24:38.610
I feel as if he was watching me.

00:24:38.610 --> 00:24:40.790
Not that he cares, not anymore.

00:24:40.790 --> 00:24:44.390
He keeps me on a leash so tight I can't breathe.

00:24:44.390 --> 00:24:48.170
He's in Long Beach, isn't he? Relax.

00:24:48.170 --> 00:24:50.860
Maybe I oughtn't to have come.

00:24:50.860 --> 00:24:52.190
Maybe you oughtn't.

00:24:52.190 --> 00:24:53.330
You want me to go?

00:24:53.330 --> 00:24:54.540
If you want to.

00:24:54.540 --> 00:24:55.870
Right now?

00:24:55.870 --> 00:24:57.080
Sure, right now.

00:25:13.760 --> 00:25:17.110
- I'm crazy about you, baby. - I'm crazy about you, Walter.

00:25:17.380 --> 00:25:19.270
The fume on your hair. What's the name of it?

00:25:19.850 --> 00:25:22.950
I don't know. I bought it in Ensenada.

00:25:23.950 --> 00:25:25.640
You ought to have some of that pink wine to go with it.

00:25:25.790 --> 00:25:28.800
The kind that bubbles. All I got is bourbon.

00:25:30.380 --> 00:25:40.550
- Bourbon is fine, Walter. - Get a couple of glasses, will you?

00:25:40.600 --> 00:25:50.470
- Soda? - Plain water, please.

00:25:50.520 --> 00:25:55.250
You know, about six months ago, a guy slipped on a cake of soap in his bathtub...

00:25:55.330 --> 00:25:56.980
...and knocked himself cold and was drowned.

00:25:57.100 --> 00:25:59.640
Only he had accident insurance.

00:26:00.190 --> 00:26:04.140
- So they had an autopsy and she didn't get away with it. - Who didn't?

00:26:04.140 --> 00:26:09.380
His wife. Then there was a case of a guy who was found shot.

00:26:09.380 --> 00:26:13.610
His wife said he was cleaning a gun and his stomach got in the way.

00:26:13.610 --> 00:26:16.820
All she collected was a three to ten stretch in Tehachapi.

00:26:16.820 --> 00:26:20.640
Perhaps it was worth it to her.

00:26:20.640 --> 00:26:24.740
See if you can carry that as far as the living room.

00:26:24.740 --> 00:26:30.000
It's nice here, Walter. Who takes care of you?

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:35.660
of it for you. Colored woman comes in a couple of times a week. Cook your own breakfast? Squeeze the

00:26:35.660 --> 00:26:40.270
grapefruit once in a while. Get the rest down at the corner drugstore. Sounds wonderful. Just

00:26:40.270 --> 00:26:46.140
strangers beside you. You don't know them and you don't hate them. You don't have to sit across the

00:26:46.140 --> 00:26:51.680
table and smile at him and that daughter of his every morning of your life. What daughter? Oh,

00:26:51.680 --> 00:26:56.390
you mean the little girl on the piano? Lola. She lives with us. He thinks a lot more of her than

00:26:56.390 --> 00:27:00.000
he does of me. You ever think of a divorce? He wouldn't give me a divorce.

00:27:00.670 --> 00:27:04.730
- I suppose because it cost him too much money. - He hasn't got any money. Not since he went into the oil business.

00:27:04.730 --> 00:27:10.110
- What he had when you married him? - Yes, he had. And I wanted a home.

00:27:10.110 --> 00:27:16.900
Why not? That's not the only reason. I was his wife's nurse. She was sick a long time.

00:27:16.900 --> 00:27:21.720
When she died, he was terribly broken up. I pitied himself.

00:27:21.720 --> 00:27:26.440
- And now you hate him? - Yes, Walter. He's so mean to me.

00:27:26.440 --> 00:27:30.000
Every time I buy a dress or a pair of shoes, he yells his head off. He never lets me go anywhere.

00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:32.660
He keeps me shut up. He's always been mean to me.

00:27:32.760 --> 00:27:37.970
Even his life insurance all goes to that daughter of his, that Lola.

00:27:38.160 --> 00:27:39.510
Nothing for you at all, huh?

00:27:39.610 --> 00:27:42.410
No, and nothing is just what I'm worth to him.

00:27:42.920 --> 00:27:46.660
So you lie awake in the dark and listen to him snore and get ideas.

00:27:47.010 --> 00:27:49.380
Walter, I don't want to kill him. I never did.

00:27:50.350 --> 00:27:53.790
Not even when he gets drunk and slaps my face.

00:27:55.880 --> 00:27:57.500
Only sometimes you wish he was dead.

00:27:58.110 --> 00:28:00.000
- Perhaps I do. - And you wish it was an accident.

00:28:00.510 --> 00:28:03.920
And you had that policy for $50,000. Is that it?

00:28:03.920 --> 00:28:07.410
Perhaps that, too.

00:28:07.410 --> 00:28:11.050
The other night we drove home from a party. He was drunk again.

00:28:11.050 --> 00:28:16.710
When we drove into the garage, he just sat there with his head on the steering wheel and the motor still running.

00:28:16.710 --> 00:28:20.930
And I thought what it would be like if I didn't switch it off.

00:28:20.930 --> 00:28:23.660
Just closed the garage doors and left them there.

00:28:23.660 --> 00:28:27.800
I'll tell you what it would be like if you had that accident policy and tried to pull a monoxide job.

00:28:27.800 --> 00:28:29.570
We've got a guy in our office named Keyes.

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:32.240
For him, a setup like that would be just like a slice of rare roast beef.

00:28:32.830 --> 00:28:34.190
In three minutes, he'd know it wasn't an accident.

00:28:34.690 --> 00:28:36.230
In ten minutes, you'd be sitting under the hot lights.

00:28:36.930 --> 00:28:38.890
In a half hour, you'd be signing your name to a confession.

00:28:39.170 --> 00:28:41.310
Well, Walter, I didn't do it, and I'm not going to do it.

00:28:41.350 --> 00:28:42.970
Not if there's an insurance company in the picture, baby.

00:28:43.370 --> 00:28:44.930
They know more tricks than a carload of monkeys.

00:28:45.540 --> 00:28:47.300
And if there's a death mixed up in it, you haven't got a prayer.

00:28:48.130 --> 00:28:50.290
They'll hang you just as sure as ten times will buy a dollar.

00:28:51.510 --> 00:28:52.860
And I don't want you to hang, baby.

00:28:55.280 --> 00:28:57.910
Stop thinking about it, will you?

00:29:02.140 --> 00:29:07.570
So we just sat there. She started crying softly like the rain on the window, and we didn't say anything.

00:29:08.750 --> 00:29:10.630
Maybe she had stopped thinking about it, but I hadn't.

00:29:12.030 --> 00:29:18.430
I couldn't, because it was all tied up with something I'd been thinking about for years, since long before I ever ran into Phyllis Dedrickson.

00:29:19.950 --> 00:29:25.380
Because you know how it is, Keyes. In this business, you can't sleep for trying to figure out all the tricks they could pull on you.

00:29:26.650 --> 00:29:30.000
You're like the guy behind the roulette wheel, watching the customers to make sure they don't cook the house.

00:29:31.820 --> 00:29:34.710
And then one night you get to thinking how you could crook the house yourself.

00:29:35.130 --> 00:29:36.080
And do it smart.

00:29:36.800 --> 00:29:38.550
Because you've got that wheel right under your hands.

00:29:38.940 --> 00:29:40.340
You know every notch in it by heart.

00:29:41.360 --> 00:29:43.350
And you figure all you need is a plant out front.

00:29:44.250 --> 00:29:45.520
A shill to put down the bed.

00:29:46.950 --> 00:29:50.130
And suddenly the doorbell rings and the whole setup is right there in the room with you.

00:29:54.590 --> 00:29:57.460
Look, Keys, I'm not trying to whitewash myself.

00:29:58.630 --> 00:29:59.730
I fought it, only I...

00:30:00.600 --> 00:30:01.990
I guess I didn't fight it hard enough.

00:30:03.690 --> 00:30:07.300
The stakes were $50,000, but they were the life of a man too.

00:30:08.030 --> 00:30:10.080
A man who'd never done me any dirt except...

00:30:11.450 --> 00:30:13.700
he was married to a woman he didn't care anything about.

00:30:14.690 --> 00:30:29.960
- And I did. - You phoned me?

00:30:31.340 --> 00:30:37.530
- Walter. - I hate him. I loathe going back to him.

00:30:37.860 --> 00:30:40.680
- You believe me, don't you, Walter? - Sure, I believe you.

00:30:43.540 --> 00:30:46.550
I can't stand it anymore. What if they did hang me?

00:30:46.690 --> 00:30:50.610
- They're not going to hang you, baby. - Better than going on this way.

00:30:50.830 --> 00:30:53.510
They're not going to hang you because you're going to do it and I'm going to help you.

00:30:53.800 --> 00:30:56.270
- Do you know what you're saying? - Sure, I know what I'm saying.

00:30:56.530 --> 00:30:58.080
We're going to do it and we're going to do it right.

00:30:58.180 --> 00:30:59.350
I'm the guy that knows how.

00:31:00.060 --> 00:31:01.280
Walter, you're hurting me.

00:31:01.390 --> 00:31:02.600
There's not going to be any slip-up.

00:31:02.750 --> 00:31:04.200
Nothing sloppy, nothing weak.

00:31:04.350 --> 00:31:05.440
It's got to be perfect.

00:31:08.120 --> 00:31:19.600
Call me tomorrow.

00:31:19.720 --> 00:31:21.220
But not from your house, from a booth.

00:31:21.320 --> 00:31:23.020
And watch your step every single minute.

00:31:23.260 --> 00:31:24.560
This is going to be perfect, you understand?

00:31:24.660 --> 00:31:26.170
Straight down the line.

00:31:28.340 --> 00:31:29.220
Down the line.

00:32:12.030 --> 00:32:16.380
That was it, Keyes. The machinery had started to move, and nothing could stop it.

00:32:17.270 --> 00:32:20.070
The first thing we had to do was fix him up with that accident policy.

00:32:21.430 --> 00:32:24.390
I knew he wouldn't buy, but all I wanted was his signature on an application.

00:32:25.640 --> 00:32:28.040
So I had to get him to sign without his knowing what he was signing.

00:32:30.030 --> 00:32:32.980
And I wanted another witness besides Phyllis to hear me give him a sales talk.

00:32:34.620 --> 00:32:38.450
I was trying to think with your brains, Keyes, because I wanted all the answers ready for all the...

00:32:38.980 --> 00:32:41.490
all the questions you were going to spring as soon as Dietrichsen was dead.

00:32:43.370 --> 00:32:44.880
A couple of nights later I went to the house.

00:32:46.410 --> 00:32:49.460
Everything looked fine except I didn't like the witness Phyllis had brought in.

00:32:51.030 --> 00:32:53.400
It was Dietrichsen's daughter, Lola.

00:32:54.190 --> 00:32:59.510
And it made me feel a little queer in the belly to have her sitting right there in the room playing Chinese checkers.

00:33:00.860 --> 00:33:02.040
As if nothing were going to happen.

00:33:04.200 --> 00:33:06.820
I suppose you realize, Mr. Diederiksen, that not being an employee,

00:33:07.280 --> 00:33:09.620
you are not covered by the State Compensation Insurance Act.

00:33:10.160 --> 00:33:12.940
The only way you can protect yourself is by having a personal policy of your own.

00:33:13.080 --> 00:33:14.260
Yeah, I know all about that.

00:33:14.320 --> 00:33:19.300
The next thing you'll tell me I need earthquake insurance and lightning insurance and hail insurance.

00:33:19.320 --> 00:33:22.900
If we bought all the insurance they could think of, we'd stay broke paying for it, wouldn't we, honey?

00:33:22.900 --> 00:33:26.940
What keeps us broke is you going out and buying five hats at a crack.

00:33:27.080 --> 00:33:28.780
Who needs a hat in California?

00:33:29.160 --> 00:33:30.000
Dollar for dollar, Mr. Diederiksen.

00:33:30.000 --> 00:33:32.400
accident insurance is the cheapest coverage you can buy.

00:33:32.400 --> 00:33:34.300
Well, maybe some other time, Mr. Neff.

00:33:34.300 --> 00:33:35.500
I had a tough day.

00:33:35.500 --> 00:33:36.360
Just as you say?

00:33:36.360 --> 00:33:39.360
Suppose we just settle that automobile insurance tonight.

00:33:39.360 --> 00:33:40.370
Sure.

00:33:40.370 --> 00:33:43.060
All we'll need on that is for you to sign the application

00:33:43.060 --> 00:33:44.060
for renewal.

00:33:44.060 --> 00:33:45.990
Fellas, do you mind if we don't finish?

00:33:45.990 --> 00:33:47.490
This game, it bores me stiff.

00:33:47.490 --> 00:33:48.990
Got something better to do?

00:33:48.990 --> 00:33:49.910
Yes, I have.

00:33:49.910 --> 00:33:53.230
Father, is it all right if I run along now?

00:33:53.230 --> 00:33:54.230
Run along where?

00:33:54.230 --> 00:33:55.230
Who with?

00:33:55.230 --> 00:33:56.230
Just Ann.

00:33:56.230 --> 00:33:57.230
We're going roller-skating.

00:33:57.230 --> 00:33:58.120
Ann who?

00:33:58.120 --> 00:33:59.130
Ann Matthews.

00:33:59.130 --> 00:34:00.000
It's not that Nino's the caddy.

00:34:00.000 --> 00:34:01.650
again, is it? It'd better not be that

00:34:01.650 --> 00:34:03.790
Sir Ketty guy. If I ever catch you with that...

00:34:03.790 --> 00:34:05.810
It's Anne Matthews, I told you, and I also

00:34:05.810 --> 00:34:07.090
told you we're going roller skating.

00:34:07.420 --> 00:34:09.480
I'm meeting her at the corner of Vermont and Franklin, the northwest

00:34:09.480 --> 00:34:11.320
corner, in case you're interested, and I'm late already.

00:34:11.990 --> 00:34:13.970
I hope that's all quite clear.

00:34:14.620 --> 00:34:16.460
Good night, Father. Good night, Phyllis.

00:34:16.580 --> 00:34:18.870
Good night, Miss Dedrickson. I'm sorry. Good night,

00:34:18.950 --> 00:34:21.070
Mr... Neff. Good night, Mr. Neff.

00:34:24.970 --> 00:34:26.390
Great little fighter for her weight.

00:34:28.320 --> 00:34:30.000
Now, if you'll just sign these, Mr. Dedrickson.

00:34:30.000 --> 00:34:32.600
- Sign what? - The application's for the order renewals.

00:34:32.700 --> 00:34:35.400
- So you'll be covered until the new policies are issued. - When will that be?

00:34:35.500 --> 00:34:38.450
- For about a week. - Just so I'm covered when I drive up north.

00:34:38.550 --> 00:34:40.440
- San Francisco? - Palo Alto.

00:34:40.540 --> 00:34:44.740
He was a Stanford man, Mr. Neff, and he still goes to his class reunion every year.

00:34:44.840 --> 00:34:48.240
What's wrong with that? Can't I have a little fun even once a year?

00:34:48.280 --> 00:34:51.180
Great football school, Stanford. Did you play football, Mr. Dietrichson?

00:34:51.280 --> 00:34:54.870
- Left guard. Almost made the varsity. Where do I sign? - The bottom line.

00:35:00.250 --> 00:35:01.050
Both copies, please.

00:35:01.470 --> 00:35:02.290
Sign twice, huh?

00:35:02.450 --> 00:35:03.390
Yes, one is the agent's copy.

00:35:03.450 --> 00:35:04.230
I need it for my files.

00:35:04.650 --> 00:35:07.010
Files, duplicates, triplicates.

00:35:10.840 --> 00:35:13.960
Thank you, Mr. Digikson.

00:35:14.500 --> 00:35:15.460
Don't worry about the check.

00:35:15.500 --> 00:35:16.860
I can pick it up at your office some morning.

00:35:17.230 --> 00:35:18.460
How much are you taking me for?

00:35:18.620 --> 00:35:19.480
$147.50.

00:35:19.820 --> 00:35:22.160
I think that's enough insurance for one evening, Mr. Neff.

00:35:22.640 --> 00:35:23.000
Plenty.

00:35:24.580 --> 00:35:29.710
Bring me some soda when you come up, Phyllis.

00:35:29.710 --> 00:35:30.000
Thank you.

00:35:30.120 --> 00:35:31.760
- Good night, Mr. Neff. - Good night, Mr. Deiderson.

00:35:31.760 --> 00:35:33.520
I think you left your hat in the hall, Mr. Neff.

00:35:33.520 --> 00:35:55.390
- Good night, Mr. Neff. - Fine.

00:35:55.390 --> 00:35:57.680
- He signed it, didn't he? - Sure, he signed it. You saw him.

00:35:57.680 --> 00:35:59.420
Listen, that trip to Palo Alto, when does he leave?

00:35:59.420 --> 00:36:00.000
End of the month.

00:36:00.000 --> 00:36:01.340
- He drives, huh? - He always drives.

00:36:01.340 --> 00:36:02.800
Not this time. You're gonna make him take the train.

00:36:02.800 --> 00:36:04.800
- Why? - Because it's all worked out for a train.

00:36:04.800 --> 00:36:07.300
Listen, baby, there's a clause in every accident policy,

00:36:07.300 --> 00:36:09.080
a little thing called double indemnity.

00:36:09.080 --> 00:36:12.050
The insurance company's put in a sort of a come-on for the customers.

00:36:12.050 --> 00:36:14.180
That means they pay double on certain accidents,

00:36:14.180 --> 00:36:16.190
the kind that almost never happen.

00:36:16.190 --> 00:36:18.680
Like, for instance, if a guy is killed on the train,

00:36:18.680 --> 00:36:20.980
they pay $100,000 instead of $50,000.

00:36:20.980 --> 00:36:22.180
I see.

00:36:22.180 --> 00:36:23.630
We're hitting it for the limit, baby.

00:36:23.630 --> 00:36:27.330
That's why it's got to be the train.

00:36:27.330 --> 00:36:29.920
It'll be the train, Walter, just the way you want it.

00:36:30.510 --> 00:36:31.510
Straight down the line.

00:36:31.510 --> 00:36:52.850
Mr. Knapp?

00:36:52.850 --> 00:36:56.910
It's me.

00:36:56.910 --> 00:36:57.830
Is anything wrong?

00:36:57.830 --> 00:36:58.830
I've been waiting for you.

00:36:58.830 --> 00:37:00.000
For me?

00:37:00.320 --> 00:37:02.740
I thought you could let me ride with you if you're going my way.

00:37:04.290 --> 00:37:09.630
Which way would that be?

00:37:10.290 --> 00:37:12.150
Down the hill, down Vermont.

00:37:12.490 --> 00:37:14.590
Oh, sure. Vermont and Franklin, northwest corner, wasn't it?

00:37:15.120 --> 00:37:16.250
Be glad to, Miss Dietrichson.

00:37:20.570 --> 00:37:23.910
Roller skating, huh? You like roller skating?

00:37:24.300 --> 00:37:25.610
I can take it or leave it.

00:37:27.900 --> 00:37:28.960
Only tonight you're leaving it.

00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:33.780
Yes, I am. I'm having a very tough time at home.

00:37:34.700 --> 00:37:37.140
My father doesn't understand me, and Phyllis hates me.

00:37:38.640 --> 00:37:39.380
Sounds tough, all right.

00:37:39.560 --> 00:37:41.240
That's why I have to lie sometimes.

00:37:42.230 --> 00:37:43.270
You mean it's not Vermont and Franklin?

00:37:43.600 --> 00:37:44.950
It's Vermont and Franklin, all right.

00:37:45.670 --> 00:37:47.010
Only it's not Ann Matthews.

00:37:48.020 --> 00:37:51.050
It's Nino's a caddy. You won't tell on me, will you?

00:37:51.780 --> 00:37:52.610
You'll have to think it over.

00:37:53.070 --> 00:37:55.930
Nino's not what my father says at all. He's just had bad luck.

00:37:56.330 --> 00:38:00.000
He was doing pre-med at USC and working nights as an usher in a theater downtown.

00:38:01.020 --> 00:38:03.360
Got behind in his credits, flunked out.

00:38:03.960 --> 00:38:06.550
Then he lost his job for talking back.

00:38:07.010 --> 00:38:08.130
So hard-headed.

00:38:09.230 --> 00:38:10.280
Comes expensive, doesn't it?

00:38:10.410 --> 00:38:12.810
Guess my father thinks nobody's good enough for his daughter.

00:38:13.010 --> 00:38:15.010
Except maybe the guy that owns Standard Oil.

00:38:15.210 --> 00:38:16.770
I wish he'd see it my way.

00:38:18.080 --> 00:38:19.300
Can't give Nino up.

00:38:20.590 --> 00:38:21.960
It'll all straighten out, Miss Dedrickson.

00:38:22.460 --> 00:38:24.040
I suppose it will sometime.

00:38:24.560 --> 00:38:26.500
Look, this is the corner right here, Mr. Neck.

00:38:29.340 --> 00:38:30.000
Nino!

00:38:31.120 --> 00:38:40.850
- Over here, Nino.

00:38:40.850 --> 00:38:42.230
This is Mr. Neff, Nino.

00:38:42.230 --> 00:38:43.720
- Hello, Nino.

00:38:43.720 --> 00:38:45.150
- The name is Zucchetti.

00:38:45.150 --> 00:38:46.310
- Nino, please.

00:38:46.310 --> 00:38:47.890
Mr. Neff gave me a ride from the house.

00:38:47.890 --> 00:38:49.110
I told him all about us.

00:38:49.110 --> 00:38:50.530
- Why does he have to get told about us?

00:38:50.530 --> 00:38:52.610
- We don't have to worry about Mr. Neff, Nino.

00:38:52.610 --> 00:38:54.180
- I'm not doing any worrying.

00:38:54.180 --> 00:38:55.640
Just don't you broadcast so much.

00:38:55.640 --> 00:38:57.320
- What's the matter with you, Nino?

00:38:57.320 --> 00:38:58.220
Well, he's a friend.

00:38:58.220 --> 00:38:59.560
- I don't have any friends.

00:39:00.000 --> 00:39:02.000
I did, I like to pick them myself. Come on.

00:39:02.000 --> 00:39:04.000
Look, Sonny, she needed a ride, so I brought her along.

00:39:04.000 --> 00:39:06.020
Is that anything you get tough about?

00:39:06.020 --> 00:39:08.860
All right, Lola, make up your mind. Are you coming or aren't you?

00:39:08.860 --> 00:39:13.760
Of course I'm coming.

00:39:13.760 --> 00:39:16.760
Don't mind him, Mr. Neff, and thanks a lot for the ride.

00:39:16.760 --> 00:39:17.680
You were awfully sweet.

00:39:17.680 --> 00:39:24.890
Nino!

00:39:24.890 --> 00:39:28.480
She was a nice kid. Maybe he was a little better than he sounded.

00:39:30.000 --> 00:39:34.370
then it gave me a nasty feeling to be thinking about them at all with that briefcase right behind

00:39:34.370 --> 00:39:40.040
my head that had her father's signature in it and what that signature meant it meant he was a dead

00:39:40.040 --> 00:39:46.650
pigeon it was only a question of time not very much time at that you know that big market up in

00:39:46.650 --> 00:39:53.720
los felis keys that's the place phyllis and i had picked for a meeting place i already had most of the

00:39:53.720 --> 00:39:58.730
plan in my head but a lot of details had to be worked out and she had to know them all by heart

00:39:58.730 --> 00:39:59.830
when the time came

00:40:01.340 --> 00:40:03.140
We had to be very careful from now on.

00:40:04.060 --> 00:40:05.860
We couldn't let anybody see us together.

00:40:06.750 --> 00:40:08.900
We couldn't even talk to each other on the telephone.

00:40:09.770 --> 00:40:11.970
Not from her house or at my office, anyway.

00:40:12.910 --> 00:40:16.680
So she was to be in the market every morning about 11 o'clock, buying stuff.

00:40:16.880 --> 00:40:20.460
And I could sort of run into her there any day I wanted to.

00:40:20.920 --> 00:40:23.670
Sort of accidentally on purpose.

00:40:24.180 --> 00:40:25.990
- Walter, I wanted to... - That's allowed.

00:40:27.870 --> 00:40:30.000
I wanted to talk to you ever since yesterday.

00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:33.290
Let me talk first. It's all set. The accident policy came through.

00:40:33.370 --> 00:40:35.830
I've got it in my pocket. I got his check, too.

00:40:35.910 --> 00:40:39.340
I saw him down in the oil fields. He thought he was paying for the auto insurance.

00:40:39.420 --> 00:40:42.210
The check's just made out to be company, so it could be for anything.

00:40:42.290 --> 00:40:44.770
But you have to send a check for the auto insurance, see?

00:40:44.850 --> 00:40:47.960
He'll be all right that way because one of the cars is in your name.

00:40:48.040 --> 00:40:56.980
Open your bag, quick. Can you get in a safe deposit box?

00:40:57.060 --> 00:40:58.980
- Yes, we both have keys. - Fine.

00:40:59.060 --> 00:41:00.000
But don't put the pocket in.

00:41:00.000 --> 00:41:03.400
policy in there yet. I'll tell you when. Remember, you never saw it. You never even

00:41:03.400 --> 00:41:07.190
touched it. Do you understand? I'm not a fool. Okay. When is he leaving on the

00:41:07.190 --> 00:41:10.290
train? That's just it. He isn't going. What? That's what I've been trying to tell you.

00:41:10.330 --> 00:41:16.540
The trip is off. What happened? Mister, could you reach me that package of baby

00:41:16.540 --> 00:41:27.070
food? That one up there? Why? They always put what I want on the top shelf. Go ahead.

00:41:27.110 --> 00:41:30.000
I'm listening. He had to fall down at the well broke his leg. It's in a cast.

00:41:30.280 --> 00:41:36.440
his leg what do we do now walter nothing we just wait wait for what until he can take the train

00:41:36.820 --> 00:41:40.570
i told you it's got to be the train but we can't wait i can't go on like this look we're not going

00:41:40.570 --> 00:41:44.140
to grab a hammer and do it quick just to get it over with there are other ways we're not going to

00:41:44.140 --> 00:41:48.300
do it other ways we can't leave it like this what do you suppose would happen if you found out about

00:41:48.300 --> 00:41:53.150
the accident policy money but not as bad as sitting in that death house we'll never talk like that

00:41:53.150 --> 00:41:57.080
don't let's start losing our heads that's all it's not our heads it's our nerve we're losing

00:42:01.210 --> 00:42:06.580
It's the waiting that's getting me.

00:42:06.580 --> 00:42:08.600
It's getting me just as bad, baby.

00:42:08.600 --> 00:42:10.720
But we've got to wait.

00:42:10.720 --> 00:42:12.680
Maybe we have, Walter, only...

00:42:12.680 --> 00:42:14.690
It's so tough without you.

00:42:14.690 --> 00:42:16.990
It's like a war between us.

00:42:16.990 --> 00:42:20.630
I'd better go, baby. I'm thinking of you every minute.

00:42:20.630 --> 00:42:30.000
After that, a full week went by and I didn't see a woman.

00:42:30.000 --> 00:42:36.830
once. I tried to keep my mind off her and off the whole idea. I kept telling myself that maybe those

00:42:36.830 --> 00:42:42.230
fates they say watch over you had gotten together and broken his leg to give me a way out. Then it

00:42:42.230 --> 00:42:49.080
was the 15th of June. You may remember that date, Keyes. He came into my office around three in the

00:42:49.080 --> 00:42:55.700
afternoon. Hello, Keyes. Just came from Norton's office. Semi-annual sales records are out. You're

00:42:55.700 --> 00:42:59.190
a high man, Walter. That's twice in a row. Congratulations. Thanks.

00:43:00.000 --> 00:43:02.340
How would you like a $50 cut in salary?

00:43:02.340 --> 00:43:05.220
Do I laugh now or will it get's funny?

00:43:05.220 --> 00:43:07.220
No, I'm serious. I've just been talking to Norton.

00:43:07.220 --> 00:43:09.530
Too much stuff piling up on my desk.

00:43:09.530 --> 00:43:10.530
Too much pressure on my nerves.

00:43:10.530 --> 00:43:12.510
I spent half the night walking up and down to my bed.

00:43:12.510 --> 00:43:14.510
I've got to happen, assistant, and I thought of you.

00:43:14.510 --> 00:43:16.490
Me? Why pick on me?

00:43:16.490 --> 00:43:18.490
Well, because I've got a crazy idea you might be good at the job.

00:43:18.490 --> 00:43:20.490
That's crazy, all right. I'm a salesman.

00:43:20.490 --> 00:43:23.410
Yeah. Peddler. Flat-hander. Back-slapper.

00:43:23.410 --> 00:43:25.620
You're too good to be a salesman.

00:43:25.620 --> 00:43:27.630
Nobody's too good to be a salesman, Alvin.

00:43:27.630 --> 00:43:30.000
All you guys do is just ring doorbells and dish out a smoothie.

00:43:30.000 --> 00:43:34.430
- What's troubling you is that 50-buck cut, isn't it? - Well, that'll trouble anybody.

00:43:34.430 --> 00:43:37.630
Now, look, Walter, the job I'm talking about takes brains and integrity.

00:43:37.630 --> 00:43:40.990
It takes more guts than there is in 50 salesmen. It's the hottest job in the business.

00:43:40.990 --> 00:43:42.990
Yeah, but it's still a desk job. I don't want to be nailed to a desk.

00:43:42.990 --> 00:43:45.600
Desk job? Is that all you can see in it?

00:43:45.600 --> 00:43:48.870
Just a hard chair to park your pants on from nine to five, huh?

00:43:48.870 --> 00:43:50.950
Just a pile of papers to shuffle around

00:43:50.950 --> 00:43:53.950
and five sharp pencils and a scratch pad to make figures on.

00:43:53.950 --> 00:43:55.950
Maybe a little doodling on the side.

00:43:55.950 --> 00:43:58.380
Well, that's not the way I look at it, Walter.

00:43:58.380 --> 00:44:00.000
To me, a claim to be honest,

00:44:00.000 --> 00:44:02.080
A claims man is a surgeon.

00:44:02.080 --> 00:44:04.080
That desk is an operating table.

00:44:04.080 --> 00:44:06.000
And those pencils are scalpels and bone chisels.

00:44:06.000 --> 00:44:08.120
And those papers are not just forms and statistics

00:44:08.120 --> 00:44:10.120
and claims for compensation.

00:44:10.120 --> 00:44:12.420
They're alive.

00:44:12.420 --> 00:44:14.460
They're packed with drama.

00:44:14.460 --> 00:44:16.610
With twisted hopes and crooked dreams.

00:44:16.610 --> 00:44:18.610
A claims man, Walter, is a doctor

00:44:18.610 --> 00:44:20.520
and a bloodhound and a...

00:44:20.520 --> 00:44:23.810
Who? Okay, hold on a minute.

00:44:23.810 --> 00:44:25.800
A claims man is a doctor and a bloodhound

00:44:25.800 --> 00:44:27.800
and a cop and a judge and a jury

00:44:27.800 --> 00:44:29.860
and a father-confessor all in one.

00:44:29.860 --> 00:44:30.000
And you want to tell me you're not into it?

00:44:30.000 --> 00:44:33.220
you don't want to work with your brains all you want to work is with your finger on the doorbell

