00:46:37.690 --> 00:46:39.090
Yeah, yeah. All right, all right.

00:46:39.290 --> 00:46:41.890
Now, what do I say to Norton? What about this job I want you for?

00:46:42.010 --> 00:46:44.640
I don't think I want it, Keyes. Thanks, it's the same.

00:46:44.830 --> 00:46:48.180
Well, fair enough. Only get this.

00:46:48.860 --> 00:46:51.640
I picked you for the job not because I think you're so darn smart,

00:46:51.810 --> 00:46:54.900
but because I thought you were a shade less dumb than the rest of the outfit.

00:47:02.270 --> 00:47:03.390
Guess I was wrong.

00:47:03.750 --> 00:47:05.000
You're not smarter, Walter.

00:47:05.220 --> 00:47:06.760
You're just a little taller.

00:47:10.280 --> 00:47:19.400
Yes, Keyes.

00:47:19.810 --> 00:47:23.140
Those fates I was talking about had only been stalling me off.

00:47:23.330 --> 00:47:25.190
Now they had thrown the switch.

00:47:25.370 --> 00:47:26.800
The gears had meshed.

00:47:26.820 --> 00:47:29.060
The time for thinking had all run out.

00:47:30.540 --> 00:47:34.420
I wanted my movements accounted for up to the last possible moment.

00:47:34.420 --> 00:47:38.460
So when I left the office, I put my rate book on the desk as if I had forgotten it.

00:47:38.460 --> 00:47:42.200
That was part of my alibi.

00:47:42.200 --> 00:47:47.630
From here on, it was a question of following the timetable, move by move.

00:47:47.630 --> 00:47:50.910
I got home about seven and drove right into the garage.

00:47:50.910 --> 00:47:55.150
This was another item to establish my alibi.

00:47:55.150 --> 00:47:58.070
- Hiya, Mr. Ned. - Hello, Charlie.

00:47:58.070 --> 00:48:00.000
How about giving the heap a wash job?

00:48:00.250 --> 00:48:04.100
want it. I got a couple of cars ahead of you. Anytime you get to it, Charlie. I'm staying

00:48:04.100 --> 00:48:08.570
in tonight. Okay, sir. Up in my apartment, I called Lou Schwartz, one of the salesmen

00:48:08.570 --> 00:48:12.780
that shared my office. He lived in Westwood, so it was a toll call and there'd be a record

00:48:12.780 --> 00:48:17.200
of it. I told him I'd forgotten my rape book and needed some dope on the public liability

00:48:17.200 --> 00:48:22.030
bond I was figuring. I changed into a navy blue suit like Dietrichson was going to wear.

00:48:23.200 --> 00:48:27.420
Lou Schwartz called me back and gave me a lot of figures. I stuffed a hand towel and

00:48:27.420 --> 00:48:30.000
a roll of adhesive into my pocket so I could fake something that looked

00:48:30.000 --> 00:48:35.700
like a cast on a broken leg. Next I stuck a card inside the telephone box so that it would fall

00:48:35.700 --> 00:48:41.760
down if the bell rang. That way I'd know if anybody had called me while I was away. Then I

00:48:41.760 --> 00:48:47.700
did the same thing to the doorbell in case anybody came to see me. I left the apartment by the service

00:48:47.700 --> 00:48:54.980
stairs. Nobody saw me. I walked all the way from my apartment to the Dietrichson house. I didn't want

00:48:54.980 --> 00:49:00.000
to take the bus because there was always the chance that someone might remember seeing me on it. I was being that

00:49:00.000 --> 00:49:06.250
careful. I could smell that honeysuckle again. Only it was even stronger now that it was night.

00:49:11.980 --> 00:49:17.430
I slid the garage door open as quietly as I could. She'd backed the sedan in just the way I told it

00:49:17.430 --> 00:49:23.670
to. I'd figured it was safer that way in case he got into the car before she drove it out. I got

00:49:23.670 --> 00:49:30.000
into the back of the car. I lay there on the floor and waited. All the time I was thinking about that

00:49:30.060 --> 00:49:34.480
dark street on the way to the station where i was to do it and the three honks on the horn

00:49:34.480 --> 00:49:40.320
that were to be the signal about 10 minutes later they came down all right honey yeah i'm all right

00:49:40.320 --> 00:49:42.160
i'll have the car out in a second

00:50:20.380 --> 00:50:22.310
- Easy, honey. We've got lots of time. - Yeah.

00:50:42.700 --> 00:50:45.960
Remember what the doctor said, if you get careless, you might end up with a shorter leg.

00:50:46.260 --> 00:50:49.420
So what? I could break the other one and match him up again.

00:50:50.280 --> 00:50:52.660
Makes you feel pretty good to get away from me, doesn't it?

00:50:52.910 --> 00:50:55.700
It's only for four days. I'll be back Monday at the latest.

00:51:12.860 --> 00:51:14.280
This is not the right street.

00:51:14.400 --> 00:51:15.640
Why did you turn here?

00:51:15.880 --> 00:51:21.220
What are you doing that for?

00:51:22.030 --> 00:51:25.510
Making the horn for me.

00:52:26.140 --> 00:52:30.000
- Will you take care of the red cap and the conductor? - Don't worry.

00:52:30.060 --> 00:52:31.100
Tell him I don't want to be helped.

00:52:31.100 --> 00:52:32.560
I said, don't worry, Walter.

00:52:32.560 --> 00:52:34.530
You start as soon as the train leaves.

00:52:34.530 --> 00:52:37.820
When you get to the refinery, turn off the highway onto the dirt road.

00:52:37.820 --> 00:52:39.780
From there, it's exactly eight-tenths of a mile to the dump

00:52:39.780 --> 00:52:40.940
beside the tracks. Remember?

00:52:40.940 --> 00:52:42.690
I remember everything.

00:52:42.690 --> 00:52:43.790
No speeding.

00:52:43.790 --> 00:52:46.780
You don't want any cops stopping you with him in the back.

00:52:46.780 --> 00:52:50.100
Walter, we've been through all that so many times.

00:52:50.100 --> 00:52:52.220
When you leave the highway, turn off all your lights.

00:52:52.220 --> 00:52:54.060
I'll be back in the observation platform.

00:52:54.060 --> 00:52:57.150
I'll drop off as close to the spot as I can.

00:52:57.150 --> 00:53:00.000
Let the train pass, then dim your eyes straight.

00:53:00.000 --> 00:53:03.000
- Twice.

00:53:03.000 --> 00:53:04.240
- San Francisco train lady?

00:53:04.240 --> 00:53:06.040
- Car nine, section 11, just my husband.

00:53:06.040 --> 00:53:07.220
- Car nine, section 11?

00:53:07.220 --> 00:53:16.720
This way, please.

00:53:16.720 --> 00:53:18.620
- Thank you, my husband doesn't like to be held.

00:53:18.620 --> 00:53:25.730
- Car number eight, up there.

00:53:25.730 --> 00:53:29.320
- Car nine, section 11, thank you.

00:53:31.180 --> 00:53:35.180
- Here's the tickets. Take good care of yourself with that leg. - Yeah, you take it easy driving home.

00:53:35.180 --> 00:53:42.160
- I'll miss you, honey. - Section 11, sir. - Thank you.

00:53:42.160 --> 00:53:52.340
- Goodbye, honey. - Good luck, honey.

00:54:01.720 --> 00:54:03.080
What, will you make of my birth right away?

00:54:03.080 --> 00:54:04.020
Yes, sir.

00:54:04.020 --> 00:54:05.760
Going back to the observation car for a smoke.

00:54:05.760 --> 00:54:06.790
Right this way, sir.

00:54:45.450 --> 00:54:52.230
My name is Jackson. I'm going all the way to Medford, Medford, Oregon.

00:54:52.230 --> 00:54:53.230
Uh-huh.

00:54:53.230 --> 00:54:55.190
I had a broken arm once.

00:54:55.190 --> 00:55:00.000
That darn cat was in the middle of the house.

00:55:00.060 --> 00:55:05.910
itch is something fierce, doesn't it? I thought I'd go crazy with mine. Palo Alto is a nice

00:55:05.910 --> 00:55:15.150
little town. You a Stanford man? I used to be. Oh, I bet you left something behind. I always

00:55:15.150 --> 00:55:20.830
do. My cigar case. I guess I left it in my overcoat back in the section. Would you care

00:55:20.830 --> 00:55:27.870
to roll yourself a cigarette, mister? Didrickson. Oh, thanks. I really prefer a cigar. Maybe

00:55:27.870 --> 00:55:30.000
the porter could... Well, I could get you a cigar.

00:55:30.120 --> 00:55:32.120
for you. Be glad to, Mr. Dietrichson.

00:55:32.120 --> 00:55:34.040
It's not too much trouble.

00:55:34.040 --> 00:55:35.860
Car 9, section 11.

00:55:35.860 --> 00:55:38.390
Car 9, section 11. A pleasure.

00:56:22.810 --> 00:56:26.790
this has got to be fast. Here, take his hat. Pick up the crutches back in the tracks.

00:56:57.290 --> 00:56:58.260
Hey, baby, that's it.

00:57:57.320 --> 00:57:57.910
All right, let's go.

00:58:08.960 --> 00:58:12.780
On the way back, we went over once more what she was to do at the inquest, if they had one.

00:58:13.400 --> 00:58:15.040
And about the insurance when that came up.

00:58:15.990 --> 00:58:18.710
I was afraid she might go to pieces a little, now that we had done it.

00:58:19.040 --> 00:58:19.910
But she was perfect.

00:58:20.720 --> 00:58:23.660
No nerves, not a tear, not even a blink of the eyes.

00:58:24.700 --> 00:58:26.520
She dropped me a block from my apartment house.

00:58:28.660 --> 00:58:28.980
Walter.

00:58:30.570 --> 00:58:32.130
What's the matter? Aren't you going to kiss me?

00:58:33.730 --> 00:58:38.600
It's straight down the line, isn't it?

00:58:40.980 --> 00:58:45.230
I love you, Walter.

00:58:46.690 --> 00:58:47.610
I love you, baby.

00:59:01.400 --> 00:59:04.060
It was two minutes past 11 as I went up the service stairs again.

00:59:04.930 --> 00:59:06.090
Nobody saw me this time either.

00:59:07.250 --> 00:59:08.610
In the apartment, I checked the bells.

00:59:08.990 --> 00:59:09.910
The cards hadn't moved.

00:59:10.640 --> 00:59:11.910
No calls, no visitors.

00:59:13.110 --> 00:59:14.650
Then I changed my clothes again.

00:59:15.750 --> 00:59:17.130
That left one last thing to do.

00:59:18.390 --> 00:59:19.530
I had to go down to the garage.

00:59:20.770 --> 00:59:22.190
I wanted Charlie to see me again.

00:59:25.880 --> 00:59:30.000
You're going to use your

00:59:30.730 --> 00:59:32.230
- Well, I'm not quite through.

00:59:32.230 --> 00:59:34.280
- Oh, that's all right, Charlie.

00:59:34.280 --> 00:59:36.280
Just going up the drugstore to get something to eat.

00:59:36.280 --> 00:59:37.480
I'm working upstairs all that way.

00:59:37.480 --> 00:59:39.300
Stomach's getting a little sore at me.

00:59:39.300 --> 00:59:42.880
- Yes, sir.

00:59:42.880 --> 00:59:44.890
- That was all there was to it.

00:59:44.890 --> 00:59:47.640
Nothing had slipped, nothing had been overlooked.

00:59:47.640 --> 00:59:50.090
There was nothing to give us away.

00:59:50.090 --> 00:59:51.930
And yet, Keyes, as I was walking down the street

00:59:51.930 --> 00:59:54.710
to the drugstore, suddenly it came over me

00:59:54.710 --> 00:59:57.310
that everything would go wrong.

00:59:57.310 --> 00:59:59.530
It sounds crazy, Keyes, but it's true, so help me.

01:00:00.540 --> 01:00:01.720
I couldn't hear my own footsteps.

01:00:06.200 --> 01:00:07.560
It was the walk of a dead man.

01:00:11.190 --> 01:00:18.250
That was the longest night I ever lived through, Keyes.

01:00:18.800 --> 01:00:20.990
And the next day was worse when the story had broken the papers.

01:00:22.730 --> 01:00:24.230
They started talking about it at the office.

01:00:24.800 --> 01:00:26.760
And the day after that, when you started digging into it,

01:00:27.760 --> 01:00:29.760
I kept my hands in my pockets because I...

01:00:30.060 --> 01:00:31.060
Thought they were shaking.

01:00:32.170 --> 01:00:34.370
I put on dark glasses so people couldn't see my eyes.

01:00:34.470 --> 01:00:36.980
Then I took them off again so they wouldn't get to wondering why I wore them.

01:00:38.350 --> 01:00:41.120
I tried to hold myself together, but...

01:00:41.500 --> 01:00:43.380
I could feel my nerves pulling me to pieces.

01:00:44.170 --> 01:00:50.230
- Oh, Walter. - Hello, Keyes.

01:00:50.270 --> 01:00:52.480
Come along. The big boss wants to see us.

01:00:52.570 --> 01:00:54.520
- The Diedertsen case? - Must be.

01:00:54.770 --> 01:00:56.960
- Anything wrong? - Well, the guy is dead.

01:00:57.060 --> 01:00:59.380
We had him assured and he's going to cost us dough. That's always wrong.

01:01:00.960 --> 01:01:03.320
- What have they got so far? - Audiency report.

01:01:03.320 --> 01:01:07.070
No heart failure, no apoplexy, no predisposing medical cause of any kind.

01:01:07.070 --> 01:01:10.000
- Died of a broken neck. - When's the inquest?

01:01:10.000 --> 01:01:13.180
Had it this morning. His wife and daughter made the identification.

01:01:13.180 --> 01:01:17.480
The train people and some of the passengers told how he went through to the observation car.

01:01:17.480 --> 01:01:19.240
It was all over in 45 minutes.

01:01:19.240 --> 01:01:22.280
Verdict? Accidental death.

01:01:22.280 --> 01:01:24.010
What do the police figure?

01:01:24.010 --> 01:01:26.850
He got tangled up in his crutches and fell off the train.

01:01:26.850 --> 01:01:29.700
They're satisfied. It's not their dough.

01:01:30.670 --> 01:01:31.670
Come on, Walter.

01:01:31.670 --> 01:01:34.700
All right, thank you very much, gentlemen.

01:01:34.700 --> 01:01:36.700
I believe the legal position is now clear.

01:01:36.700 --> 01:01:38.680
Please stand by, I may need you later.

01:01:38.680 --> 01:01:40.340
Come in, Mr. Keyes.

01:01:40.340 --> 01:01:42.060
You too, Mr. Neff.

01:01:42.060 --> 01:01:49.050
Do you find this an uncomfortably warm day, Mr. Keyes?

01:01:49.050 --> 01:01:52.050
Oh, I'm sorry, Mr. Norton, but I didn't know this was formal.

01:01:52.050 --> 01:01:53.050
Sit down, gentlemen.

01:01:53.050 --> 01:01:54.040
Thank you.

01:01:54.040 --> 01:01:54.960
Any new developments?

01:01:54.960 --> 01:01:56.960
Well, I just talked to this Jackson long distance up in Medford, Oregon.

01:01:56.960 --> 01:01:57.960
Who's Jackson?

01:01:57.960 --> 01:01:59.960
He's the last man who saw Dietrichson alive.

01:02:00.030 --> 01:02:02.110
They were out on the observation platform together talking.

01:02:02.770 --> 01:02:06.290
Dietrichsen wanted a cigar, and so Jackson went back to get Dietrichsen's cigar case for him.

01:02:06.490 --> 01:02:08.630
When he returned to the observation platform, no Dietrichsen.

01:02:09.210 --> 01:02:12.510
Well, Jackson didn't think anything was wrong until a wire caught up with the train to Santa Barbara.

01:02:12.940 --> 01:02:15.470
They found Dietrichsen's body on the tracks near Burbank.

01:02:15.770 --> 01:02:17.850
Very interesting about the cigar case.

01:02:18.330 --> 01:02:18.850
Anything else?

01:02:19.230 --> 01:02:20.190
No, not much.

01:02:20.590 --> 01:02:22.950
Dietrichsen's secretary says she didn't know anything about the policy.

01:02:23.240 --> 01:02:28.850
There's a daughter, but all she remembers is left talking to her father about accident insurance at the house one night.

01:02:29.200 --> 01:02:30.000
I couldn't sell them at first.

01:02:30.710 --> 01:02:31.790
Mr. Dietrichson opposed it.

01:02:32.170 --> 01:02:32.930
He said he'd think it over.

01:02:33.660 --> 01:02:36.500
Later, I saw him in the oil fields and closed him.

01:02:36.930 --> 01:02:38.370
He signed the application and gave me his check.

01:02:38.840 --> 01:02:41.320
A fine piece of salesmanship that was, Mr. Neff.

01:02:41.540 --> 01:02:43.100
Well, there's no sense in pushing Neff around.

01:02:43.160 --> 01:02:44.740
He's got the best sales record in the office.

01:02:45.270 --> 01:02:47.710
Are your salesmen supposed to know a customer is going to fall off the train?

01:02:48.330 --> 01:02:49.240
Fall off a train?

01:02:49.950 --> 01:02:51.950
Are we sure Dietrichson fell off the train?

01:02:52.570 --> 01:02:53.410
I don't get it.

01:02:53.820 --> 01:02:54.680
You don't, Mr. Keyes.

01:02:55.120 --> 01:02:56.460
Then what do you think of this case?

01:02:56.460 --> 01:02:58.310
This policy might cost us a great deal of money.

01:02:58.430 --> 01:03:00.000
As you know, it contains a double indemnity.

01:03:00.000 --> 01:03:05.360
clause. Just what is your opinion? No opinion at all. Not even a hunch? One of those interesting

01:03:05.360 --> 01:03:10.750
little hunches of yours? No, not even a hunch. I'm surprised, Mr. Keyes. I formed a very definite

01:03:10.750 --> 01:03:16.470
opinion. I think I know. In fact, I know I know what happened to Dietrichson. You know you know

01:03:16.470 --> 01:03:29.910
what? I know it was not an accident. What do you say to that? Me? Well, you've got the ball. Let's

01:03:30.000 --> 01:03:32.630
that just because a man has a large office, he...

01:03:32.630 --> 01:03:36.080
Yes?

01:03:36.080 --> 01:03:38.580
Have her come in, please.

01:03:38.580 --> 01:03:41.680
There's a widespread feeling that just because a man has a large office,

01:03:41.680 --> 01:03:42.960
he must be an idiot.

01:03:42.960 --> 01:03:45.490
I'm having a visitor, if you don't mind.

01:03:45.490 --> 01:03:47.860
No, no, I want you to stay and watch me handle this.

01:03:47.860 --> 01:03:49.720
Mrs. Dietrichson.

01:03:49.720 --> 01:03:56.820
Thank you very much for coming, Mrs. Dietrichson.

01:03:56.820 --> 01:03:58.550
I assure you, I appreciate it.

01:03:58.550 --> 01:04:00.000
Mr. Keyes.

01:04:00.440 --> 01:04:30.000
here. How do you do? Mr. Neff. I've met Mr. Neff. How do you do? Mrs. Dietrichson. Why don't you sit down? May I extend our sympathy in your bereavement? I hesitated before asking you to come here so soon after your loss, but now that you're here, I hope you won't mind if I plunge straight into business. You know why we ask you to come, don't you? No, all I know is that your secretary made it sound very urgent. Your husband had an accident policy with this company. Evidently, you don't know that, Mrs. Dietrichson. No, I remember some talk at the house, but he didn't seem to want it. Your husband took the policy out a few days later.

01:04:30.000 --> 01:04:31.430
Mr. Diederson.

01:04:31.430 --> 01:04:33.830
You'll probably find the policy among his personal effects.

01:04:33.830 --> 01:04:35.730
His state deposit box hasn't been opened yet.

01:04:35.730 --> 01:04:38.290
It seems a tax examiner has to be present.

01:04:38.290 --> 01:04:41.090
Please, Miss Dixon, I don't want you to think you're being subjected to any questioning,

01:04:41.090 --> 01:04:42.910
but there are a few things we should like to know.

01:04:42.910 --> 01:04:44.400
What sort of things?

01:04:44.400 --> 01:04:46.620
We have the report of the coroner's inquest.

01:04:46.620 --> 01:04:48.060
Accidental death.

01:04:48.060 --> 01:04:49.660
We're not entirely satisfied.

01:04:49.660 --> 01:04:54.330
In fact, we're not satisfied at all.

01:04:54.330 --> 01:04:59.090
Frankly, Miss Diederson, we suspect suicide.

01:05:02.650 --> 01:05:11.880
- I'm sorry, would you like a glass of water? - Please. Thank you.

01:05:17.710 --> 01:05:21.620
Had your husband been depressed or moody lately, Miss Dietrichson?

01:05:21.990 --> 01:05:23.840
Did he have financial worries, for instance?

01:05:24.250 --> 01:05:28.130
He was perfectly all right, and I don't know of any financial worries.

01:05:28.150 --> 01:05:30.000
Let us examine this so-called accident.

01:05:30.480 --> 01:05:33.280
First, your husband takes out this policy in absolute secrecy.

01:05:33.750 --> 01:05:34.070
Why?

01:05:34.560 --> 01:05:37.150
Because he doesn't want his family to suspect what he intends to do.

01:05:37.560 --> 01:05:38.080
Do what?

01:05:38.280 --> 01:05:40.180
Next, he goes on this trip entirely alone.

01:05:40.300 --> 01:05:41.080
He has to be alone.

01:05:41.460 --> 01:05:43.760
He hobbles all the way out to the observation platform.

01:05:44.020 --> 01:05:47.300
Very unlikely with his leg in the cast, unless he has a very strong reason.

01:05:47.950 --> 01:05:49.990
Once there, he finds he's not alone.

01:05:50.110 --> 01:05:50.910
There's a man there.

01:05:51.170 --> 01:05:52.070
What was his name, Keith?

01:05:52.370 --> 01:05:53.370
His name was Jackson.

01:05:53.650 --> 01:05:54.690
Probably still is.

01:05:55.210 --> 01:05:59.010
So he gets rid of this Jackson with some flimsy excuse about cigars.

01:05:59.270 --> 01:06:00.000
And then he's alone.

01:06:00.570 --> 01:06:02.500
- And then he doesn't. - Does what?

01:06:02.500 --> 01:06:05.340
He jumps. Suicide. In which case, the company is not liable.

01:06:05.340 --> 01:06:09.320
You know that, of course. Now, we could go to court.

01:06:09.320 --> 01:06:12.470
I don't know anything. In fact, I don't know why I came here.

01:06:12.470 --> 01:06:16.310
Just a moment, please. I said we could go to court. I didn't say we want to.

01:06:16.310 --> 01:06:19.390
What I want to suggest is a compromise on both sides.

01:06:19.390 --> 01:06:22.220
A settlement for a certain sum, a part of the policy value.

01:06:22.220 --> 01:06:26.230
Don't bother, Mr. Norton. When I came in here, I had no idea you owed me any money.

01:06:26.230 --> 01:06:30.000
You told me you did, then you told me you didn't. Now you tell me you want to pay me a part of it.

01:06:30.000 --> 01:06:33.080
Whatever it is. You want to bargain with me at a time like this.

01:06:33.100 --> 01:06:36.400
I don't like your insinuations about my husband and I don't like your methods.

01:06:36.480 --> 01:06:39.250
In fact, I don't like you, Mr. Norton.

01:06:39.270 --> 01:06:40.440
Goodbye, gentlemen.

01:06:41.860 --> 01:06:47.730
Nice going, Mr. Norton. You sure carry that ball.

01:06:47.870 --> 01:06:49.910
What do you fumble on the goal line?

01:06:50.060 --> 01:06:53.520
Then you heaved an illegal forward pass and got thrown for a 40-yard loss.

01:06:53.540 --> 01:06:56.000
Now you can't pick yourself up because you haven't got a leg to stand on.

01:06:56.080 --> 01:06:59.130
Why haven't he? She can go to court and we can prove it was suicide.

01:06:59.210 --> 01:07:00.000
Can we?

01:07:00.800 --> 01:07:04.150
Mr. Norton, the first thing that struck me was that suicide angle.

01:07:04.850 --> 01:07:07.590
Only I dumped it into the waste paper basket just three seconds later.

01:07:08.660 --> 01:07:12.180
You know, you ought to take a look at these statistics on suicide sometime.

01:07:12.620 --> 01:07:14.500
You might learn a little something about the insurance business.

01:07:14.640 --> 01:07:16.800
Mr. Keyes, I was raised in the insurance business.

01:07:16.960 --> 01:07:18.180
Yeah, in the front office.

01:07:18.810 --> 01:07:21.490
Come now, you've never read an actuarial table in your life, have you?

01:07:22.050 --> 01:07:23.830
Why, they've got ten volumes on suicide alone.

01:07:24.360 --> 01:07:28.090
Suicide by race, by color, by occupation, by sex, by seasons of the year, by time of day.

01:07:28.330 --> 01:07:30.000
Suicide, how committed, by poisons.

01:07:30.000 --> 01:07:34.180
by firearms, by drowning, by leaps, suicide by poison, subdivided by types of poison,

01:07:34.460 --> 01:07:38.560
such as corrosive, irritant, systemic, gaseous, narcotic, alkaloid, protein, and so forth.

01:07:38.780 --> 01:07:42.580
Suicide by leaps, subdivided by leaps from high places, under the wheels of trains, under

01:07:42.580 --> 01:07:45.580
the wheels of trucks, under the feet of horses, from steamboats.

01:07:45.990 --> 01:07:50.540
But Mr. Norton, of all the cases on record, there's not one single case of suicide by

01:07:50.540 --> 01:07:52.080
leap from the rear end of a moving train.

01:07:52.570 --> 01:07:55.030
And do you know how fast that train was going at the point where the body was found?

01:07:55.680 --> 01:07:56.720
15 miles an hour.

01:07:57.180 --> 01:08:00.000
Now, how can anybody jump off a slow-moving train like that with any kind of exercise?

01:08:00.060 --> 01:08:02.430
with the expectation that he would kill himself.

01:08:02.510 --> 01:08:07.180
No, no soap, Mr. Norton. We're sunk and we'll have to pay through the nose, and you know it.

01:08:07.270 --> 01:08:18.600
May I have this? Come on, Walter. Next time I'll rent a tuxedo.

01:08:35.530 --> 01:08:39.030
I could have hugged you right then and there, Keyes, you and your statistics.

01:08:39.630 --> 01:08:44.590
You were the only one we were really scared of, and instead you were almost playing on our team.

01:08:45.520 --> 01:08:47.860
That evening when I got home, my nerves had eased off.

01:08:48.550 --> 01:08:52.540
I could feel the ground under my feet again, and it looked like easy going from there on in.

01:08:52.950 --> 01:08:58.040
That 100,000 bucks looked as safe for Phyllis and me as if we had the check already deposited in the bank.

01:09:04.920 --> 01:09:07.440
Hello. Oh, hello, baby.

01:09:08.200 --> 01:09:11.500
Sure, everything's fine. You were wonderful in Norton's office.

01:09:11.690 --> 01:09:14.180
I felt so funny, I wanted to look at you all the time.

01:09:15.110 --> 01:09:17.350
How'd you think I felt, baby? Where are you?

01:09:17.760 --> 01:09:21.510
At the drugstore, just a block away. Can I come up?

01:09:21.580 --> 01:09:23.810
Okay, but be careful. Don't let anybody see you.

01:09:51.020 --> 01:09:58.610
What's on your mind?

01:10:00.000 --> 01:10:05.040
leg. The guy had a broken leg. What are you talking about? I'm talking about Dietrichson.

01:10:05.040 --> 01:10:10.670
He had accident insurance, didn't he? Yeah. Then he broke his leg, didn't he? So what?

01:10:10.670 --> 01:10:15.510
And he didn't put in a claim. Why didn't he put in a claim? Why? What are you driving

01:10:15.510 --> 01:10:22.010
at? Walter, I had dinner two hours ago, and he's stuck halfway. Little man of yours is

01:10:22.010 --> 01:10:25.600
acting up again, huh? There's something wrong with the Dietrichson case. Why, because he

01:10:25.600 --> 01:10:30.000
didn't file a claim? Maybe he just didn't have time. Maybe he just didn't know that

01:10:30.000 --> 01:10:38.080
he was insured no no that couldn't be it uh you delivered the policy to him personally didn't you

01:10:38.080 --> 01:10:46.190
yeah you got his check sure i did had any bicarbonate of soda no i haven't walter i've been

01:10:46.190 --> 01:10:50.870
living with this little man for 26 years he's never failed me yet there's got to be something

01:10:50.870 --> 01:10:57.650
wrong well maybe norton was right maybe it was suicide no not suicide but not an accident either

01:10:57.650 --> 01:10:59.570
what else

01:11:00.320 --> 01:11:05.390
Now, look, Walter. A guy takes out an accident policy that's worth $100,000 if he's killed on the train.

01:11:05.470 --> 01:11:07.310
And two weeks later, he is killed on the train.

01:11:07.390 --> 01:11:11.260
And not in a train accident, mind you, but falling off some silly observation car.

01:11:11.480 --> 01:11:13.970
You know what the mathematical probability of that is?

01:11:14.170 --> 01:11:17.400
One out of, oh, I don't know how many billions.

01:11:17.860 --> 01:11:19.560
And after that, the broken leg.

01:11:20.130 --> 01:11:22.760
Now, it just... it just can't be the way it looks.

01:11:22.970 --> 01:11:27.400
- Something has been worked on us. - Such as what?

01:11:35.240 --> 01:11:38.250
Don't you have any peppermint or something?

01:11:38.250 --> 01:11:40.200
Oh, sorry. Want a little soda water?

01:11:40.200 --> 01:11:41.200
No, no, no.

01:11:41.200 --> 01:11:44.170
Who do you suspect?

01:11:44.170 --> 01:11:46.170
Well, maybe I like to make things easy for myself,

01:11:46.170 --> 01:11:48.170
but I always tend to suspect the beneficiary.

01:11:48.170 --> 01:11:49.320
You mean the wife?

01:11:49.320 --> 01:11:52.280
Yeah, that wide-eyed dame that just didn't know anything about anything.

01:11:52.280 --> 01:11:54.270
Why are you crazy, Key? She wasn't even on the train.

01:11:54.270 --> 01:11:55.270
I know she wasn't, Walter.

01:11:55.270 --> 01:11:57.250
I don't claim to know how it was worked or who worked it,

01:11:57.250 --> 01:11:59.190
but all I know is that it was worked.

01:12:00.350 --> 01:12:03.670
I've got to get to a drugstore. This thing feels like a hunk of concrete inside me.

01:12:05.040 --> 01:12:05.560
Good night, Walter.

01:12:06.080 --> 01:12:06.750
Good night, Keyes.

01:12:07.240 --> 01:12:09.080
See you at the office in the water.

01:12:09.320 --> 01:12:09.540
Yeah.

01:12:11.400 --> 01:12:14.230
I'd like to move in on a right now, tonight.

01:12:14.710 --> 01:12:17.430
It wasn't for Norton and the Stripe Pants' ideas about company policy.

01:12:18.950 --> 01:12:21.410
I'd have the police after her so fast that they'd make a head spin.

01:12:21.750 --> 01:12:25.130
They'd put her through the wringer and bother the things they would squeeze out.

01:12:26.050 --> 01:12:28.070
Well, you haven't got a single thing to go on, Keyes.

01:12:28.320 --> 01:12:30.000
Oh, not too much. Just $26.

01:12:30.000 --> 01:12:33.760
She has experience, all the percentage there is, and there's a hug of concrete in my stomach.

01:12:36.740 --> 01:12:38.100
Any one of those things?

01:12:45.510 --> 01:12:50.760
Good night, Keys.

01:12:51.640 --> 01:12:52.140
So long, Walter.

01:13:06.200 --> 01:13:09.170
- How much does he know? - He doesn't know anything.

01:13:10.140 --> 01:13:13.660
- It's those stinking hunches of his. - He can't prove anything, can he?

01:13:13.760 --> 01:13:15.650
Not if we're careful, not if we don't see each other for a while.

01:13:16.050 --> 01:13:21.900
- How long a while? - Until this dies down.

01:13:22.610 --> 01:13:25.480
You don't know Keyes. Once he gets his teeth into something, he never lets go.

01:13:25.750 --> 01:13:28.360
He'll investigate you. He'll have you shadowed. He'll watch you every minute from now on.

01:13:30.090 --> 01:13:34.740
- Afraid, baby? - Yes, I'm afraid, not of keys.

01:13:34.930 --> 01:13:38.350
I'm afraid of us. We're not the same anymore.

01:13:38.550 --> 01:13:41.550
We did it so we could be together, but instead of that, it's pulling us apart, isn't it, Walter?

01:13:41.750 --> 01:13:47.600
- What are you talking about? - And you don't really care whether we see each other or not.

01:13:54.220 --> 01:13:59.760
- Take all rest. Good afternoon. - Hello, Mr. Neff.

01:14:01.120 --> 01:14:03.040
- Hello. - Lola Dietrichsen.

01:14:03.430 --> 01:14:06.320
- Don't you remember me? - Yes. Yes, of course.

01:14:06.490 --> 01:14:08.360
Could I talk with you just a few minutes?

01:14:08.750 --> 01:14:10.950
Somewhere where we could be alone.

01:14:11.440 --> 01:14:13.730
Oh, yes, sir. Come into my office.

01:14:20.000 --> 01:14:23.430
- Something about what happened? - Yes, Mr. Neff.

01:14:24.370 --> 01:14:28.310
- It's about my father's death. - I'm terribly sorry, Miss Dietrichsen.

01:14:32.270 --> 01:14:34.690
Oh, Lou, do you mind if I use the office alone for a few minutes?

01:14:34.870 --> 01:14:35.790
No, it's all yours, Bowler.

01:14:38.460 --> 01:14:46.150
I mean, Miss Jeanette, I'm not crazy.

01:14:46.680 --> 01:14:47.550
I'm not hysterical.

01:14:47.800 --> 01:14:48.820
I'm not even crying.

01:14:49.910 --> 01:14:52.050
But I have the awful feeling that something's wrong.

01:14:53.620 --> 01:14:57.010
And I had that same feeling once before, when my mother died.

01:14:57.640 --> 01:14:58.320
When your mother died?

01:14:58.600 --> 01:14:59.620
We were at Lake Owlhead.

01:15:00.030 --> 01:15:05.420
it was six years ago we had a cabin there it was winter and very cold my mother was very sick with

01:15:05.420 --> 01:15:12.480
pneumonia she had a nurse with her there were just three of us in the cabin one night i got

01:15:12.480 --> 01:15:17.870
up and went into my mother's room she was delirious with fever all the bed covers were on the floor

01:15:17.870 --> 01:15:22.690
and the windows were wide open the nurse wasn't in the room i ran and covered my mother up as

01:15:22.690 --> 01:15:30.000
quickly as i could just then i heard the door open behind me the nurse stood there she didn't

01:15:30.000 --> 01:15:33.630
I didn't say a word, but there was a look in her eyes.

01:15:33.630 --> 01:15:37.130
I'll never forget.

01:15:37.130 --> 01:15:40.340
Two days later, my mother was dead.

01:15:43.560 --> 01:15:45.580
Do you know who that nurse was?

01:15:45.580 --> 01:15:48.160
- No, who? - Phyllis.

01:15:48.160 --> 01:15:50.660
I tried to tell my father, but I was just a kid then.

01:15:50.660 --> 01:15:52.180
He wouldn't listen to me.

01:15:52.180 --> 01:15:55.100
Six months later, she married him, and I kind of talked myself

01:15:55.100 --> 01:15:58.270
out of the idea she could have done anything like that.

01:15:58.270 --> 01:15:59.800
But now it's all back again.

01:16:00.000 --> 01:16:02.720
I know that something's happened to my father, too.

01:16:03.060 --> 01:16:06.740
You're not making sense, Miss Diederson. Your father fell off a train.

01:16:06.940 --> 01:16:09.890
Yes. Two days before he fell off that train, what was Phyllis doing?

01:16:10.030 --> 01:16:13.630
She was in a room in front of a mirror with a black hat on, pinning a black veil to it.

01:16:13.830 --> 01:16:16.230
As if she couldn't wait to see how she would look in mourning.

01:16:17.440 --> 01:16:21.040
You've had a pretty bad job, Miss Diederson. Aren't you just imagining these things?

01:16:21.150 --> 01:16:25.210
I caught her eyes in the mirror. They had that look in them they had before my mother died.

01:16:25.590 --> 01:16:27.260
That same look.

01:16:28.200 --> 01:16:29.480
You don't like your stepmother, do you?

01:16:30.120 --> 01:16:31.640
Isn't it just because she is your stepmother?

01:16:31.760 --> 01:16:33.240
I loathe her, because she did it.

01:16:33.440 --> 01:16:34.300
She did it for the money.

01:16:34.620 --> 01:16:36.320
Only you're not going to pay her, are you, Mr. Neff?

01:16:36.600 --> 01:16:38.320
She's not going to get away with it this time,

01:16:38.360 --> 01:16:39.460
because I'm going to speak up.

01:16:39.700 --> 01:16:41.160
I'm going to tell everything I know.

01:16:41.400 --> 01:16:42.480
You'd better be careful saying things.

01:16:42.480 --> 01:16:43.340
I'm not afraid.

01:16:44.530 --> 01:17:00.000
Sorry, I didn't mean to act like that.

01:17:02.460 --> 01:17:06.900
- All this that you've been telling me, who else have you told? - No one.

01:17:07.000 --> 01:17:12.480
- How about your stepmother? - Of course not. I've moved out.

01:17:13.090 --> 01:17:15.030
I'm not living at home anymore.

01:17:15.200 --> 01:17:19.590
- And you haven't told that boyfriend of yours, Cicchetti? - I'm not seeing him anymore.

01:17:19.690 --> 01:17:23.950
- We had a fight. - Where are you living now?

01:17:24.290 --> 01:17:29.180
- I dropped myself a little apartment in Hollywood. - Four walls and you just sit and look at 'em, huh?

01:17:31.440 --> 01:17:32.440
Yes, Mr. Neff.

01:17:35.790 --> 01:17:40.290
So that evening, I took her to dinner at a Mexican restaurant down on O'Bara Street, where nobody would see us.

01:17:41.030 --> 01:17:42.030
I wanted to cheer her up.

01:17:43.410 --> 01:17:46.210
The next day was Sunday, and we went for a ride down to the beach.

01:17:46.950 --> 01:17:47.850
She'd loosened up a bit.

01:17:48.140 --> 01:17:49.140
She was even laughing.

01:17:50.000 --> 01:17:53.000
I had to make sure that she wouldn't tell that stuff about Phyllis to anybody else.

01:17:53.320 --> 01:17:55.700
It was dynamite, whether it was true or not.

01:17:56.630 --> 01:17:58.230
I had no chance to talk to Phyllis.

01:17:58.360 --> 01:18:00.000
You were watching her like a hawk, Keys.

01:18:00.570 --> 01:18:03.070
I couldn't even phone her because I was afraid you had the wires tapped.

01:18:05.080 --> 01:18:07.980
Monday morning there was a note on my desk that you wanted to see me, Keyes.

01:18:09.180 --> 01:18:10.980
For a minute I wondered if it could be about Lola.

01:18:11.570 --> 01:18:12.890
It was worse.

01:18:14.360 --> 01:18:17.320
Outside your door was the last guy in the world I wanted to see.

01:18:19.800 --> 01:18:28.700
Come here, come here, Lola.

01:18:30.860 --> 01:18:31.420
Hello, Keyes.

01:18:32.840 --> 01:18:33.640
I want to ask you something.

01:18:34.690 --> 01:18:37.750
After all the years we've known each other, do you mind if I make a rather blunt statement?

01:18:38.140 --> 01:18:38.640
About what?

01:18:38.760 --> 01:18:39.220
About me.

01:18:40.460 --> 01:18:42.370
Walter, I'm a very great man.

01:18:42.880 --> 01:18:43.060
Yeah?

01:18:43.140 --> 01:18:43.980
This Dietrich's in business.

01:18:44.620 --> 01:18:47.150
It's murder, and murders don't come any meter.

01:18:48.020 --> 01:18:50.560
As fancy a piece of homicide as anybody ever ran into.

01:18:50.960 --> 01:18:52.660
Smart, tricky, almost perfect.

01:18:52.820 --> 01:18:56.060
But I think Papa has it all figured out.

01:18:56.210 --> 01:19:00.000
Figured out and wrapped up in tissue paper with pink ribbons on it.

01:19:00.540 --> 01:19:01.960
Go ahead, I'm listening.

01:19:02.080 --> 01:19:06.940
You know what? That guy, Dietrichsen, was never on the train!

01:19:07.770 --> 01:19:10.960
- He wasn't? - No, he wasn't.

01:19:11.420 --> 01:19:16.910
Now look, Walter, you can't be sure of killing a man by throwing him off a train that's going 15 miles an hour.

01:19:16.990 --> 01:19:21.290
The only way you can be sure is to kill him first, and then throw his body on the tracks.

01:19:21.510 --> 01:19:26.560
Now that would mean either killing him on the train, or, and this is where it really gets fancy,

01:19:26.650 --> 01:19:29.860
you kill him somewhere else and put him on the tracks. Two possibilities.

01:19:30.410 --> 01:19:31.890
And I personally by the second.

01:19:32.560 --> 01:19:34.560
You're way ahead of me, Keyes.

01:19:35.140 --> 01:19:36.320
Well, look, Walter, it was like this.

01:19:37.010 --> 01:19:40.720
They killed a guy, the wife, and a somebody else.

01:19:41.410 --> 01:19:43.730
Then the somebody else took the crutches and went on the train as Dietrichson.

01:19:44.410 --> 01:19:45.690
Then the somebody else jumped off.

01:19:46.240 --> 01:19:48.400
Then they put the body on the tracks where the train had passed.

01:19:48.940 --> 01:19:50.040
An impersonation, see?

01:19:50.940 --> 01:19:51.680
Singed to work.

01:19:52.350 --> 01:19:53.010
Because it was night.

01:19:53.440 --> 01:19:54.460
Very few people were about.

01:19:54.640 --> 01:19:55.820
They had the crutches to stare at.

01:19:56.220 --> 01:19:57.840
They never really looked at the man at all.

01:19:58.490 --> 01:19:59.960
Fancy, all right, Keyes, but...

01:20:00.030 --> 01:20:01.030
Maybe it's a little too fancy.

01:20:01.030 --> 01:20:02.110
Is it?

01:20:02.110 --> 01:20:04.110
I tell you, it all fits together like a watch.

01:20:04.110 --> 01:20:06.830
Now, let's see what we have in the way of proof.

01:20:06.830 --> 01:20:10.660
The only guy who really got a good look at this supposed Dietrichsen is sitting right

01:20:10.660 --> 01:20:11.660
outside my office.

01:20:11.660 --> 01:20:14.030
I took the trouble to bring him down here from Oregon.

01:20:14.030 --> 01:20:18.190
Come here, Mr. Jackson.

01:20:18.190 --> 01:20:20.080
Yes, sir, Mr. Keyes.

01:20:20.080 --> 01:20:22.080
These are fine cigars you smoke.

01:20:22.080 --> 01:20:23.080
Two for a quarter.

01:20:23.080 --> 01:20:24.070
That's what I said.

01:20:24.070 --> 01:20:25.070
Well, did you study those photographs?

01:20:25.070 --> 01:20:26.070
Yes, indeed.

01:20:26.070 --> 01:20:28.080
I studied them thoroughly, very thoroughly.

01:20:28.080 --> 01:20:30.000
Have you made up your mind?

01:20:30.000 --> 01:20:31.500
I'm a Medford man, Medford, Oregon.

01:20:31.500 --> 01:20:33.780
Up in Medford, we take our time making up our minds.

01:20:33.780 --> 01:20:34.920
Well, we're not in Medford now.

01:20:34.920 --> 01:20:35.910
We're in a hurry.

01:20:35.910 --> 01:20:37.160
Let's have it.

01:20:37.160 --> 01:20:39.220
Are these photographs of the late Mr. Dietrichson?

01:20:39.220 --> 01:20:40.720
Yes.

01:20:40.720 --> 01:20:42.150
Then my answer is no.

01:20:42.150 --> 01:20:43.020
What do you mean, no?

01:20:43.020 --> 01:20:45.340
I mean, this is not the man that was on the train.

01:20:45.340 --> 01:20:46.480
Will you swear to that?

01:20:46.480 --> 01:20:48.540
Mr. Keyes, I'm a Medford man, Medford, Oregon.

01:20:48.540 --> 01:20:49.360
If I say it, I mean it.

01:20:49.360 --> 01:20:51.230
If I mean it, of course, I'll swear it.

01:20:51.230 --> 01:20:53.250
Hey, while Walter, there's your proof.

01:20:53.250 --> 01:20:54.650
Oh, this is Mr. Neff, one of our salesmen.

01:20:54.650 --> 01:20:56.610
Oh, pleased to meet you, Mr. Neff, please indeed.

01:20:56.610 --> 01:20:57.070
How are you?

01:20:57.070 --> 01:20:59.670
- Very fine, thank you. Never a bit better. - Sit down, Mr. Jackson.

01:21:00.350 --> 01:21:03.170
Just how would you describe the man you saw on the observation platform?

01:21:03.590 --> 01:21:06.330
Well, I'm pretty sure he was a younger man.

01:21:06.600 --> 01:21:08.860
About 10 or 15 years younger than the man in these photographs.

01:21:09.690 --> 01:21:11.030
Peterson was about 50, wasn't he, Walter?

01:21:11.550 --> 01:21:12.970
51, according to the policy.

01:21:14.060 --> 01:21:16.300
The man I saw was nothing like 51 years old.

01:21:17.170 --> 01:21:19.250
Of course, it was pretty dark out on that platform.

01:21:19.840 --> 01:21:21.720
Come to think of it, he tried to keep his back towards me.

01:21:22.250 --> 01:21:23.590
But I'm positive just the same.

01:21:23.940 --> 01:21:24.640
Well, thank you, Jackson.

01:21:25.090 --> 01:21:26.980
Of course, you understand this man is strictly confidential.

01:21:27.400 --> 01:21:30.000
We may need you again down here in Los Angeles if the case comes to come.

01:21:30.000 --> 01:21:34.130
court. Anytime you need me, I'm entirely at your disposal, gentlemen. Expenses paid, of course.

01:21:34.370 --> 01:21:42.980
Oh, yes, yes. Of course. Get me Lubin in the cashier's office. Hello, Lubin. This is Keyes.

01:21:43.720 --> 01:21:48.690
Listen, I'm sending a man named Jackson down to you with an expense account. Well, we brought him

01:21:48.690 --> 01:21:54.660
down here from Medford, Oregon, in connection with the Dietrichson Clay. Uh-huh. Well, take care of

01:21:54.660 --> 01:21:59.220
his hotel, will you? Ever been in Oregon, Mr. Neff? Yeah, coming right down. I've never been up there.

01:22:00.090 --> 01:22:02.240
Oh, wait a minute. You go trout fishing?

01:22:02.460 --> 01:22:06.120
- Maybe I saw you up Klamath Falls way. - No, I don't fish.

01:22:06.220 --> 01:22:07.810
I've never been fishing, Mr. Neff.

01:22:07.910 --> 01:22:08.960
Neff. Neff.

01:22:09.080 --> 01:22:10.020
It's the name.

01:22:10.190 --> 01:22:12.360
There's a family of Neffs in Corvallis.

01:22:12.540 --> 01:22:13.330
No relation.

01:22:13.430 --> 01:22:15.330
Let me see. This man's an automobile dealer in Corvallis.

01:22:15.430 --> 01:22:16.830
A very reputable man, too, I'm told.

01:22:16.930 --> 01:22:19.230
All right, Mr. Jackson, suppose you go down to the cashier's office.

01:22:19.330 --> 01:22:20.830
Room 27 on the 11th floor.

01:22:20.930 --> 01:22:23.730
He'll take care of your expense account and your ticket for the train tonight.

01:22:23.830 --> 01:22:24.690
Tonight?

01:22:25.070 --> 01:22:26.640
Tomorrow morning would suit me better.

01:22:26.850 --> 01:22:30.000
There's a very good osteopath in town I'd like to see before I leave.

01:22:30.920 --> 01:22:34.660
- Hostiopath. - Well, just don't put it around the expense account, hmm?

01:22:34.660 --> 01:22:38.580
- Well, goodbye, gentlemen. - It's been a pleasure.

01:22:38.580 --> 01:22:43.400
- Goodbye. - Well, there it is, Walter.

01:22:43.400 --> 01:22:45.970
It's beginning to come apart at the seams already.

01:22:45.970 --> 01:22:48.920
Murder's never perfect. Always comes apart, sooner or later.

01:22:48.920 --> 01:22:51.560
And when two people are involved, it's usually sooner.

01:22:51.560 --> 01:22:54.780
Now, we know the Dietrichson dame is in it and a somebody else.

01:22:54.780 --> 01:22:56.630
Pretty soon we'll know where that somebody else is.

01:22:56.630 --> 01:22:57.750
He'll show, he's got to show.

01:22:57.750 --> 01:22:59.260
Sometime, somewhere, they've got to meet.

01:22:59.260 --> 01:23:00.000
Their emotions are all different.

01:23:00.000 --> 01:23:04.700
kicked up whether it's love or hate doesn't matter they can't keep away from each other they may

01:23:04.700 --> 01:23:09.150
think it's twice as safe because they're two of them but it isn't twice as safe it's ten times

01:23:09.150 --> 01:23:14.180
twice as dangerous they've committed a murder and it's not like taking a trolley ride together where

01:23:14.180 --> 01:23:17.540
they can get off at different stops they're stuck with each other they've got to ride all the way

01:23:17.540 --> 01:23:23.400
to the end of the line that's a one-way trip and the last stop is the cemetery she put in her claim

01:23:23.400 --> 01:23:26.440
i'm gonna throw it right back at her

01:23:32.680 --> 01:23:34.320
Let her sue us if she dares.

01:23:34.480 --> 01:23:37.110
I'll be ready for her, and that somebody else.

01:23:37.170 --> 01:23:39.090
They'll be digging their own graves.

01:23:39.120 --> 01:23:50.930
Mr. Dedrickson, this is Jerry's Market.

01:23:51.180 --> 01:23:54.000
We just got in a shipment of that English soap you were asking about.

01:23:54.000 --> 01:23:55.280
Will you be coming by today?

01:23:56.170 --> 01:23:57.430
Thank you, Mr. Dedrickson.

01:24:31.080 --> 01:24:32.080
What's the matter?

01:24:32.080 --> 01:24:36.200
Everything's the matter.

01:24:36.200 --> 01:24:37.200
Keys is rejecting your claim.

01:24:37.200 --> 01:24:40.500
He's sitting back with his mouth watering, waiting for you to sue.

01:24:40.500 --> 01:24:41.560
He wants you to sue, but you're not going to.

01:24:41.560 --> 01:24:42.560
What's he got to stop me?

01:24:42.560 --> 01:24:43.560
He's got plenty.

01:24:43.560 --> 01:24:49.610
He's figured out how it was worked.

01:24:49.610 --> 01:24:50.690
He knows there was somebody else on the train,

01:24:50.690 --> 01:24:52.690
and he's dug up a witness he thinks can prove it.

01:24:52.690 --> 01:24:53.690
Prove it how?

01:24:53.690 --> 01:24:54.690
If he rejects that claim, I have to sue.

01:24:54.690 --> 01:24:55.690
Yeah, and then you'll end up in court,

01:24:55.690 --> 01:24:57.650
and a lot of other things are going to come up.

01:24:57.650 --> 01:25:00.000
Like, for instance, about you and the police.

01:25:00.030 --> 01:25:03.150
What about me and the first Mrs. Dietrichsen?

01:25:03.150 --> 01:25:05.150
The way she died.

01:25:05.150 --> 01:25:08.230
And about that black hat you were trying on, before you needed a black hat.

01:25:08.230 --> 01:25:11.120
Lola's been telling you some of her cockeyed stories. She's been seeing you.

01:25:11.120 --> 01:25:14.120
I've been seeing her, if you want to know, so she won't yell her head off about what she knows.

01:25:14.120 --> 01:25:17.120
She's putting on a knack for you, crying all over your shoulder, that lion.

01:25:17.120 --> 01:25:19.120
Keep her out of this. All I'm telling you is we're not going to sue.

01:25:19.120 --> 01:25:23.120
Because you don't want the money anymore, even if you could have it because she's made you feel like a heel all of a sudden?

01:25:23.120 --> 01:25:26.120
It isn't the money anymore. It's our necks. We're pulling out, do you understand?

01:25:26.120 --> 01:25:28.100
Because of what Keys can do?

01:25:28.100 --> 01:25:30.000
You're not fooling me, Wolf.

01:25:30.440 --> 01:25:32.660
It's because of Lola, what you did to her father.

01:25:32.740 --> 01:25:35.400
You're afraid she might find out someday. You can't take it, can you?

01:25:35.480 --> 01:25:43.250
- I said leave her out of this. - It's me on door.

01:25:43.800 --> 01:25:46.150
- I don't want to be left out of it. - Stop saying that.

01:25:46.160 --> 01:25:48.530
It's just that it hasn't worked out as we wanted. We can't go through with it.

01:25:48.610 --> 01:25:51.450
We have gone through with it, Walter. The tough part is all behind us.

01:25:51.530 --> 01:25:54.290
We just have to hold on now and not go soft inside.

01:25:54.370 --> 01:25:56.400
Stick close together the way we started out.

01:25:56.560 --> 01:25:57.520
Watch it.

01:26:18.780 --> 01:26:21.160
I wasn't going to do anything about it, not until I met you.

01:26:22.230 --> 01:26:23.430
You claimed the whole thing.

01:26:24.430 --> 01:26:25.870
I only wanted him dead.

01:26:26.380 --> 01:26:27.940
And I'm the one that fixed it so he was dead.

01:26:29.130 --> 01:26:29.840
Is that what you're telling me?

01:26:30.960 --> 01:26:32.180
And nobody's pulling out.

01:26:33.040 --> 01:26:35.540
We went into this together and we're coming out at the end together.

01:26:35.660 --> 01:26:37.360
It's straight down the line for both of us.

01:26:38.740 --> 01:26:39.100
Remember?

01:26:42.130 --> 01:26:47.120
Yes, I remember it.

01:26:48.160 --> 01:26:51.000
Just like I remembered what you had told me, Keyes.

01:26:51.960 --> 01:26:53.660
About that trolley car ride.

01:26:55.510 --> 01:26:57.330
How there was no getting off till the end of the line.

01:26:59.080 --> 01:27:00.000
Where the cemetery was.

01:27:02.360 --> 01:27:04.700
And then I got to thinking what cemeteries are for.

01:27:05.750 --> 01:27:07.680
They're to put dead people in.

01:27:10.280 --> 01:27:12.780
I guess that was the first time I ever thought about Phyllis that way.

01:27:13.520 --> 01:27:14.690
Dead, I mean.

01:27:15.950 --> 01:27:18.540
And how it would be if she were dead.

01:27:19.210 --> 01:27:22.620
I saw Lola three or four times that week.

01:27:23.360 --> 01:27:26.660
One night we went up into the hills behind the Hollywood Bowl.

01:27:27.900 --> 01:27:29.860
I guess it sounds crazy, Keith, but...

01:27:30.160 --> 01:27:32.980
It was only with her that I could relax and let go a little.

01:28:03.680 --> 01:28:11.920
- Why are you crying? But tell me, huh? - Of course I will, Walter.

01:28:12.000 --> 01:28:16.240
I wouldn't tell anybody else but you. It's about Nino.

01:28:16.600 --> 01:28:19.860
- Sir Kitty, what about him? - They killed my father together.

01:28:19.970 --> 01:28:24.090
He and Phyllis. He helped her do it. I know he did.

01:28:24.350 --> 01:28:27.740
- What makes you say that? - I've been following him.

01:28:27.820 --> 01:28:29.560
He's been to her house night after night.

01:28:30.000 --> 01:28:35.880
was Phyllis and him all along. Maybe he was just going with me as a blind in the night

01:28:35.880 --> 01:28:39.060
of the murder. He promised me you weren't going to talk like this anymore. He was supposed

01:28:39.060 --> 01:28:46.600
to pick me up after a lecture at UCLA, but he never showed up. He said he was sick. Sick!

01:28:47.760 --> 01:29:00.000
Couldn't show up because the train was leaving with my father on. Maybe I'd just

01:29:00.000 --> 01:29:02.920
Isn't that crazy? Maybe it's all on my mind.

01:29:03.890 --> 01:29:05.050
Sure, it's all on your mind.

01:29:05.910 --> 01:29:10.240
I only wish it were Walter, 'cause I still love him.

01:29:16.290 --> 01:29:19.720
Zachetti. Phyllis and Zachetti.

01:29:21.380 --> 01:29:22.820
What was he doing up at her house?

01:29:24.340 --> 01:29:25.640
I couldn't figure that one out.

01:29:27.140 --> 01:29:29.090
I tried to make sense out of it and got nowhere.

01:29:30.600 --> 01:29:32.920
But the real brain twister came the next day.

01:29:34.100 --> 01:29:35.570
You sprang it on me, Keyes.

01:29:35.980 --> 01:29:39.800
After office hours, when you caught me down in the lobby of the building.

01:29:40.150 --> 01:29:42.040
Oh, Walter. Walter, just a minute.

01:29:43.630 --> 01:29:49.840
- No, Keyes? - Just hang on to your hat, Walter.

01:29:50.080 --> 01:29:52.180
- What for? - Oh, nothing much.

01:29:52.320 --> 01:29:54.770
That Dietrichson case just busted wide open.

01:29:55.100 --> 01:29:58.770
- How do you mean? - Well, the guy showed, that's how.

01:30:00.000 --> 01:30:02.090
- What guy? - The guy who helped her do it.

01:30:02.210 --> 01:30:04.360
- The, uh, somebody else? - Mm-hmm.

01:30:04.740 --> 01:30:07.360
- No kidding. - Yeah, she just filed suit against us.

01:30:07.560 --> 01:30:09.050
It's okay by me.

01:30:09.130 --> 01:30:12.140
When we get him in that courtroom, I'll tear him to pieces, both of 'em.

01:30:12.460 --> 01:30:14.480
- Come on, I'll buy you a marty, Walter. - No, thanks, Keyes.

01:30:14.560 --> 01:30:16.760
- With two olives. - No, no, I've got to get a shave and a shoe shine.

01:30:16.820 --> 01:30:18.730
I've got a date. - Margie.

01:30:18.810 --> 01:30:21.610
I still bet she drinks from the bottle.

01:30:21.980 --> 01:30:23.440
They give you matches when you buy cigars, you know.

01:30:23.500 --> 01:30:24.540
All you have to do is ask for 'em.

01:30:24.600 --> 01:30:26.580
Don't like 'em. They always explode in my pocket.

01:30:26.640 --> 01:30:27.520
So long, Walter.

01:30:33.200 --> 01:30:35.340
I was scared stiff, Keeze.

01:30:35.590 --> 01:30:38.340
Maybe you were playing cat and mouse with me.

01:30:38.420 --> 01:30:41.920
Maybe you knew all along I was the somebody else.

01:30:42.000 --> 01:30:46.230
I had to find out, and I knew where to look in your office.

01:31:18.680 --> 01:31:20.480
Memo to Mr. Norton. Confidential.

01:31:20.730 --> 01:31:21.470
Dietrichson file.

01:31:22.440 --> 01:31:25.040
With regard to your proposal to put Walter Neff under surveillance,

01:31:25.520 --> 01:31:26.600
I disagree absolutely.

01:31:27.530 --> 01:31:29.760
I have investigated his movements on the night of the crime,

01:31:29.840 --> 01:31:30.000
and he's...

01:31:34.160 --> 01:31:39.650
In addition to this, I have known Neff intimately for eleven years, and I have personally vouched for him without reservation.

01:31:46.780 --> 01:31:57.340
Furthermore, whatsoever has been established between Walter Neff and his fellow Steve, whereas I am now able to report that such a connection has been established between her and another man.

01:31:58.330 --> 01:31:59.880
This man has been observed.

01:32:01.210 --> 01:32:05.350
on the nights of July 9th, 10th, 11th, 12th, and 13th.

01:32:05.350 --> 01:32:09.260
We have succeeded in identifying him as one Nino Zaccetti,

01:32:09.260 --> 01:32:11.730
former medical student, age of 28,

01:32:11.730 --> 01:32:14.140
residing at Lilac Court Apartments,

01:32:14.140 --> 01:32:17.510
1228 and a half North La Brea Avenue.

01:32:17.510 --> 01:32:19.530
We have checked Zaccetti's movements

01:32:19.530 --> 01:32:21.140
on the night of the crime

01:32:21.140 --> 01:32:24.400
and have found that they cannot be accounted for.

01:32:24.400 --> 01:32:27.670
I am preparing a more detailed report for your consideration.

01:32:27.670 --> 01:32:30.000
It is my belief that we already have

01:32:30.090 --> 01:32:36.220
evidence against Aketty and Mrs. Dietrichson to justify police action. I strongly urge that this

01:32:36.220 --> 01:32:44.120
whole matter be turned over to the office of the district attorney. Respectfully, Barton Keyes.

01:33:20.860 --> 01:33:23.300
to see her tonight.

01:33:23.300 --> 01:33:26.100
Yes, it has to be tonight.

01:33:26.100 --> 01:33:28.520
How's 11 o'clock?

01:33:28.520 --> 01:33:29.700
I don't worry about keys.

01:33:30.250 --> 01:33:32.310
Just leave the front door unlocked and put the lights out.

01:33:33.360 --> 01:33:34.770
No, nobody is watching the house.

01:33:35.110 --> 01:33:36.170
Not anymore.

01:33:36.830 --> 01:33:38.110
It's just for the neighbors.

01:33:39.330 --> 01:33:41.390
I told you not to worry about Keyes.

01:33:41.950 --> 01:33:43.060
I'll see you at 11 o'clock.

01:33:44.230 --> 01:33:45.420
Yeah.

01:33:46.630 --> 01:33:48.340
Goodbye, baby.

01:33:52.100 --> 01:33:55.410
I guess I don't have to tell you what I intended to do at 11 o'clock, Keyes.

01:33:56.470 --> 01:33:59.580
For the first time I saw a way to get clear of the whole mess I was in.

01:34:00.190 --> 01:34:02.370
And of Phyllis, too, all at the same time.

01:34:03.000 --> 01:34:04.870
Yeah, that's what I thought.

01:34:05.750 --> 01:34:08.450
What I didn't know was that she had plans of her own.

01:35:09.020 --> 01:35:30.000
- In here, Walter. - A thousand and a half.

01:35:30.760 --> 01:35:34.290
- Nobody. Why? - What's that music?

01:35:35.010 --> 01:35:46.180
- Radio up the street. - Just like the first time I came here, isn't it?

01:35:46.360 --> 01:35:49.650
We were talking about automobile insurance.

01:35:50.970 --> 01:35:55.830
Only you were thinking about murder. I was thinking about that anklet.

01:35:56.660 --> 01:35:59.800
- And what are you thinking about now? - I'm all through thinking, baby.

01:36:01.020 --> 01:36:05.180
- I just came to say good-bye. - Good-bye? Where are you going?

01:36:05.280 --> 01:36:10.490
You're the one that's going, baby, not me. I'm getting off the trolley car, right at this corner.

01:36:10.590 --> 01:36:13.080
Suppose you stop being fancy. Let's have it, whatever it is.

01:36:13.180 --> 01:36:17.550
All right, I'll tell you. A friend of mine's got a funny theory.

01:36:17.650 --> 01:36:21.350
He says when two people commit a murder, it's sort of like they're riding on a trolley car together.

01:36:21.450 --> 01:36:24.190
One can't get off without the other. They're stuck with each other.

01:36:24.290 --> 01:36:27.380
They have to go on riding together, clear to the end of the line.

01:36:27.480 --> 01:36:30.000
The last stop is a cemetery.

01:36:30.000 --> 01:36:32.050
- He's got something there. - You bet he has.

01:36:32.940 --> 01:36:34.840
Two people are going to ride at the end of the line, all right.

01:36:35.680 --> 01:36:36.880
Only I'm not going to be one of them.

01:36:37.630 --> 01:36:39.480
I've got another guy to finish my ride for me.

01:36:40.140 --> 01:36:43.030
- Who are you talking about? - An acquaintance of yours.

01:36:43.790 --> 01:36:44.820
A Mr. Cicchetti.

01:36:45.950 --> 01:36:49.670
Come on, baby, I just got into this thing because I happened to know a little something about insurance, didn't I?

01:36:49.960 --> 01:36:53.470
I was a sucker. I'd have been brushed off just as soon as you got your hands on the money.

01:36:53.670 --> 01:36:56.430
- Nobody wanted to brush you off. - Save it, I'm telling this.

01:36:56.780 --> 01:36:59.600
- It's been you and that Cicchetti guy all along, hasn't it? - That's not true.

01:37:00.670 --> 01:37:03.130
It doesn't make any difference if it's true or not.

01:37:05.000 --> 01:37:07.430
The point is, Keyes believes Cicchetti is the one he's been looking for.

01:37:08.110 --> 01:37:11.040
He'll have him in that gas chamber before he knows what's happened to him.

01:37:11.500 --> 01:37:13.440
What's happening to me all this time?

01:37:13.820 --> 01:37:21.860
Don't be silly, baby. What do you think's gonna happen to you?

01:37:22.410 --> 01:37:24.110
You helped him do the murder, didn't you?

01:37:24.590 --> 01:37:27.720
That's what Keyes thinks, and what's good enough for Keyes is good enough for me.

01:37:28.110 --> 01:37:30.000
Maybe it's not good enough for me, Walter. Maybe I don't know.

01:37:30.000 --> 01:37:36.120
- I don't go for the idea. Maybe I'd rather talk. - Sometimes people are aware they can't talk.

01:37:36.220 --> 01:37:41.680
Under six feet of dirt, maybe. And if it was you, they'd charge that up to Cicchetti too, wouldn't they?

01:37:41.780 --> 01:37:47.160
Sure they would. And that's just what's gonna happen, baby.

01:37:47.260 --> 01:37:51.550
Because he's coming here tonight in about 15 minutes. With the cops right behind him.

01:37:51.570 --> 01:37:56.950
- It's all taken care of. - That would make everything lovely for you, wouldn't it?

01:37:57.050 --> 01:38:00.000
Right. It's gotta be done before they suit you yourself.

01:38:00.000 --> 01:38:03.770
Lola gets a chance to sound off before they trip you up in the stand

01:38:03.770 --> 01:38:04.770
and you start to go in and drag me down with you.

01:38:04.770 --> 01:38:07.770
Maybe I'd stick it here so they won't get a chance to trip me up,

01:38:07.770 --> 01:38:08.770
so we can get the money and be together.

01:38:08.770 --> 01:38:10.850
That's cute. Say it again.

01:38:10.850 --> 01:38:12.560
He came here first to ask where Lola was.

01:38:12.560 --> 01:38:14.560
I made him come back. I was working on him.

01:38:14.560 --> 01:38:16.800
He's a crazy sort of guy, quick-tempered.

01:38:16.800 --> 01:38:18.940
I kept hammering into him that she was with another man

01:38:18.940 --> 01:38:20.940
so I'd go into one of his jealous ragers

01:38:20.940 --> 01:38:22.940
and then I'd tell him where she was.

01:38:22.940 --> 01:38:25.880
And you know what he would have done to her, don't you, Walter?

01:38:25.880 --> 01:38:30.000
Yeah. And for once I believe you, because it's just right here.

01:38:30.000 --> 01:38:35.390
- We're both rotten. - Only you're a little more rotten.

01:38:35.440 --> 01:38:37.680
You got me to take care of your husband for you.

01:38:37.790 --> 01:38:40.770
And then you get Cicchetti to take care of Alola. Maybe take care of me too.

01:38:40.790 --> 01:38:43.850
Then somebody else would have come along to take care of Cicchetti for you.

01:38:43.940 --> 01:38:47.220
- That's the way you operate, isn't it, baby? - Suppose it is.

01:38:47.340 --> 01:38:49.360
Is what you've got cooked up for tonight any better?

01:38:51.120 --> 01:38:55.350
I don't like that music anymore. Mind if I close the window?

01:39:15.930 --> 01:39:17.250
You're better than that, can't you, baby?

01:39:17.250 --> 01:39:19.780
Try it again.

01:39:19.780 --> 01:39:24.680
Maybe if I came a little closer.

01:39:36.110 --> 01:39:38.770
How's this? Think you can do it now?

01:39:38.770 --> 01:39:52.330
Shoot again, baby.

01:39:52.330 --> 01:39:58.160
Don't tell me it's because you've been in love with me all this time.

01:40:00.090 --> 01:40:02.600
No, I never loved you, Walter. Not you or anybody else.

01:40:02.600 --> 01:40:06.210
I'm rotten to the heart. I used you just as you said.

01:40:06.210 --> 01:40:11.560
Not all you ever meant to me, until a minute ago,

01:40:11.560 --> 01:40:15.040
when I couldn't fire that second shot.

01:40:15.040 --> 01:40:19.270
I never thought that could happen to me.

01:40:19.270 --> 01:40:22.700
Sorry, baby. I'm not buying.

01:40:22.700 --> 01:40:25.220
I'm not asking you to buy. Just hold me close.

01:40:32.010 --> 01:40:32.980
Goodbye, baby.

01:41:28.320 --> 01:41:28.770
Eddie.

01:41:38.830 --> 01:41:43.990
I said, "Come here. My name is Neff."

01:41:44.270 --> 01:41:47.470
Yeah, and I still don't like it. What do you want?

01:41:48.010 --> 01:41:51.440
Look, kid, I want to give you a present.

01:41:51.880 --> 01:41:54.970
- There's a nice new nickel. - What's a gag?

01:41:55.020 --> 01:41:57.520
Suppose you go on back down the hill to the drugstore and make a phone call.

01:41:57.610 --> 01:41:59.730
Keep your nickel and buy yourself an ice cream cone.

01:42:00.000 --> 01:42:01.540
The number is granted, 0386.

01:42:01.750 --> 01:42:02.850
That's for Miss Diederiksen.

01:42:03.070 --> 01:42:04.000
Her first name is Lola.

01:42:04.290 --> 01:42:05.430
She isn't with a nickel.

01:42:06.030 --> 01:42:07.970
If I ever talk to her, it's not going to be over any telephone.

01:42:08.280 --> 01:42:09.060
Tough, aren't you?

01:42:09.200 --> 01:42:10.920
Here, take the nickel and call her. She wants you to.

01:42:11.340 --> 01:42:13.190
She doesn't want any part of me.

01:42:13.540 --> 01:42:14.440
I know who told you that.

01:42:14.620 --> 01:42:15.360
It's not true.

01:42:15.990 --> 01:42:17.700
Lola's in love with you. She always has been.

01:42:18.610 --> 01:42:20.530
Don't ask me why. I couldn't even guess.

01:42:20.950 --> 01:42:22.950
Here, granted 0386.

01:42:23.150 --> 01:42:24.360
Now go on and call her.

01:42:24.880 --> 01:42:25.760
Go on, that way.

01:42:37.130 --> 01:42:38.250
4.30 now, Kase.

01:42:39.540 --> 01:42:45.280
But if she's still lying alone up there in that house,

01:42:46.400 --> 01:42:48.130
what have they found her by now?

01:42:48.890 --> 01:42:51.690
I told her a lot of things.

01:42:51.920 --> 01:42:53.770
They don't matter anymore.

01:42:58.380 --> 01:43:00.000
Except I want you to do a favor for me.

01:43:00.000 --> 01:43:06.470
I want you to be the one to tell Lola kind of gently before it breaks wide open.

01:43:06.470 --> 01:43:11.100
And I want you to take care of her and that guy, Cicchetti,

01:43:11.100 --> 01:43:19.360
so he doesn't get pushed around too much.

01:43:19.360 --> 01:43:22.730
Hello, Keyes.

01:43:33.450 --> 01:43:34.610
Pretty early, aren't you?

01:43:34.610 --> 01:43:38.740
I was wondering what time you got down to the office.

01:43:38.740 --> 01:43:43.120
How did that little man of yours pull you out of bed?

01:43:43.120 --> 01:43:45.470
The janitor did.

01:43:45.470 --> 01:43:49.100
Seems you leaked a little blood on the way in here.

01:43:49.100 --> 01:43:53.470
Yeah, wouldn't be surprised.

01:43:53.470 --> 01:43:57.270
Wanted to straighten you out on that Dietrichson case.

01:43:57.270 --> 01:43:59.060
So I gather.

01:44:01.210 --> 01:44:02.310
How long have you been standing there?

01:44:02.680 --> 01:44:03.520
Long enough.

01:44:04.160 --> 01:44:07.410
Kind of a crazy story with a crazy twist to it.

01:44:07.430 --> 01:44:09.950
One you didn't quite figure out.

01:44:09.960 --> 01:44:12.390
Well, you can't figure them all, Walter.

01:44:13.120 --> 01:44:15.360
That's right. I guess you can't at that.

01:44:16.400 --> 01:44:19.950
Now, I suppose I get the big speech.

01:44:21.290 --> 01:44:23.240
One with all the two-dollar words in it.

01:44:23.240 --> 01:44:26.630
Let's have it, Keith.

01:44:27.980 --> 01:44:29.320
Walter?

01:44:30.830 --> 01:44:31.990
You're all washed up.

01:44:32.970 --> 01:44:36.380
Thanks, Keyes. I was short, anyway.

01:44:36.400 --> 01:44:39.820
I'm gonna call for a doctor.

01:44:39.830 --> 01:44:45.490
For? So they can patch me up?

01:44:45.900 --> 01:44:48.310
So they can nurse me along till I get back on my feet?

01:44:48.740 --> 01:44:51.800
So I can walk into that gas chamber of San Quentin on my own power?

01:44:51.800 --> 01:44:53.490
Is that it, Keyes?

01:44:53.700 --> 01:44:55.280
Something like that.

01:44:55.390 --> 01:44:57.970
Right. I got a different idea.

01:44:58.190 --> 01:44:58.960
Yeah?

01:44:59.210 --> 01:45:00.000
Look, Keyes.

01:45:00.990 --> 01:45:02.340
Suppose you went back to bed.

01:45:02.390 --> 01:45:05.290
Didn't find these cylinders till tomorrow morning when the office opens.

01:45:05.860 --> 01:45:07.680
After that, you can play it any way you like.

01:45:08.410 --> 01:45:10.700
Would you do that much for me, Keyes?

01:45:11.550 --> 01:45:12.970
Give me one good reason.

01:45:13.380 --> 01:45:15.140
I need four hours to get where I'm going.

01:45:15.380 --> 01:45:18.480
- You're not going anywhere, Walter. - You bet I am.

01:45:19.360 --> 01:45:22.310
- I'm going across the border. - You haven't got a chance, Walter.

01:45:22.410 --> 01:45:25.600
- Good enough to try for. - You'll never make the border.

01:45:26.110 --> 01:45:27.110
That's what you think.

01:45:27.890 --> 01:45:29.120
Watch me.

01:45:30.190 --> 01:45:31.890
He'll never even make the elevator.

01:45:32.720 --> 01:45:33.720
So long, Keys.

01:46:03.260 --> 01:46:09.450
Hello. Uh, send an ambulance to the Pacific Building on Olive Street.

01:46:09.450 --> 01:46:11.040
Yeah. Police.

01:46:31.180 --> 01:46:33.880
- How you doing, Walter? - Fine.

01:46:35.410 --> 01:46:38.610
Somebody moved the elevator a couple of miles away.

01:46:39.810 --> 01:46:43.660
They're on the way.

01:46:46.220 --> 01:46:48.350
You know why you couldn't figure this one, Keyes?

01:46:48.910 --> 01:46:52.920
I'll tell you, because the guy you were looking for was too close.

01:46:54.260 --> 01:46:58.740
- Right across the desk from you. - Closer than that, Walter.

01:47:03.260 --> 01:47:05.830
I love you too.
